const FACTS := {
	"gold_1933_saint_gaudens_double_eagl": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "20 United States dollars"], ["WEIGHT", "33.431 g"], ["DIAMETER", "34.1 mm (1.342 in)"], ["EDGE", "lettered \"E PLURIBUS UNUM\""], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper; 99.99% gold (2009)"], ["GOLD", ".96750 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Saint-Gaudenz double eagle is a twenty- dollar gold coin, or double eagle, produced by the United States Mint from 1907 to 1933. The coin is named after its designer, the sculptor Augustus Saint-Gaudenz, who designed the obverse and reverse."], ["THE STORY", "The Mint had offered only a small prize to the winner, and all invited artists (including Saint-Gaudenz himself) refused to submit entries."], ["MINTING", "Despite a mintage of almost 1.8 million pieces of the 1929 double eagle, it is estimated that fewer than 2,000 exist today, with all the rest melted by the government in the late 1930s."], ["THE DESIGN", "Saint-Gaudenz had produced the models for these dies under the misapprehension that the first pieces had been struck on a production press, rather than on the Mint's only medal press, and therefore only slight adjustment need be made."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Fewer than a thousand are known to have survived; one, in almost-perfect condition (graded MS -67) sold in 2005 for $287,500. In 2002 this coin sold at auction by Sotheboys for $7,590,020."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "These patterns are today known as the \"Ultra High Relief\" or \"Extra High Relief\" pieces, and only about 20 are known —one sold in a 2005 auction for $2,990,000."], ["ONE MORE THING", "According to numismatic historian Roger Burdette, the first 1933 double eagles were struck on March 2, 1933."]], "year": 1907},
	"silv_1794_flowing_hair_silver_dolla": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1 Dollar United States dollar"], ["DIAMETER", "39–40 mm (1.53–1.57 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "2 mm"], ["EDGE", "Lettered"], ["METAL", "copper, silver"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1794–1795"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Flowing Hair dollar was the first dollar coin issued by the United States federal government. The coin was minted in 1794 and 1795; its size and weight were based on the Spanish dollar, which was popular in trade throughout the Americas."], ["THE STORY", "On January 28, 1791, Treasury Secretary Alexander Hamilton presented a report to Congress detailing the findings of a study he had conducted on the monetary system and the potential of a United States mint."], ["MINTING", "The new coinage press was completed in early 1795, and the first group of dollars, totalling 3,810 coins, was delivered on May 6. The law, known as the Coinage Act of 1792, was signed into law on April 2, 1792, by President Washington."], ["THE DESIGN", "Extra care was taken during the engraving of this denomination, because the dollar would be the largest American coin, and would thus receive the most scrutiny from foreign nations."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 2013, the finest known example, which was among the earliest coins struck and was prepared with special care, was sold at auction for $10,016,875, the highest selling price of any coin in history."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "On March 3, 1794, Congress lowered the bonds to $5,000 and $1,000 for chief coiner and assayer, respectively."], ["ONE MORE THING", "It was previously sold in 2010 for what was then a record sum of $7.85 million, to the Cardinal Collection Educational Foundation."]], "year": 1794},
	"silv_1804_draped_bust_dollar": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1.00 U.S. dollars"], ["WEIGHT", "26.96 g"], ["DIAMETER", "39–40 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "2 mm"], ["EDGE", "Lettered"], ["METAL", "copper, silver"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Draped Bust dollar is a United States dollar coin minted from 1795 to 1803, and was reproduced, dated 1804, into the 1850s. The design succeeded the Flowing Hair dollar, which began mintage in 1794 and was the first silver dollar struck by the United States Mint."], ["THE STORY", "The Coinage Act of 1792 called for the silver coinage to be struck in an alloy consisting of 89.2% silver and 10.8% copper."], ["MINTING", "Bullion deposits continued to decline, and in 1797, silver dollar production reached the lowest point since 1794 with a mintage of just 7,776 pieces."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 1867, one of the original 1804 dollars was sold at auction for $750 ($ 16,350 today). Seven years later, on November 27, 1874, a specimen sold for $700 ($ 18,851 today)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The coins continued to gain popularity throughout the 20th century, and the price reached an all-time high in 1999, when an example graded Proof-68 was sold at auction for $4,140,000."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In November 1797, the Bank deposited approximately $30,000 worth of French silver."]], "year": 1795},
	"gold_1787_brasher_doubloon": {"stats": [["DIRECTOR", "John Brahm and"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Brasher Doubloon (known in the UK as The High Window) is a 1947 American crime film noir directed by John Brahm and starring George Montgomery and Nancy Guild."], ["MINTING", "Film rights were bought in May 1942 by 20th Century-Fox, who used it as the basis of a script for Time to Kill (1942), a movie in their B-picture series about Michael Shayne."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Chandler's popular 'shamus' and, we might add, his efforts to recover the stolen brasher doubloon, a rare coin with a violent history, is the least of his exploits to date."]], "year": 0},
	"cent_1913_liberty_head_nickel": {"stats": [["METAL", "nickel"], ["FIRST OUT", "February 1913"], ["SOLD", "3 million"], ["BIG SALE", "$3 million"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The 1913 Liberty Head nickel is an American five-cent piece which was produced in extremely limited quantities unauthorized by the United States Mint, making it one of the best-known and most coveted rarities in American numismatics."], ["THE STORY", "If true, this was not a unique occurrence; such clandestine strikes were quite common in the 19th century, with the Class II and III 1804 silver dollars perhaps the best-known instance."], ["MINTING", "These were the first official strikings of nickels in 1913, since the United States Mint's official records list no Liberty Head nickels produced in that year."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 1972, one specimen of the five cent coin became the first coin to sell for over US$100,000; in 1996, another specimen became the first to sell for over US$1 million."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "A specimen was sold for US$3 million in a 2004 private sale, then resold for US$3.7 million at a public auction in 2010."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The coin was sold at auction by the heirs in April 2013 for US$3,172,500, significantly above an estimated value of US$2,500,000."]], "year": 1913},
	"gold_1822_capped_bust_half_eagle": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The Capped Bust coinage of the United States consisted of a half dime, dime, quarter and half dollar."], ["THE STORY", "The Turban design was used on the gold Quarter Eagle, Half Eagle, and Eagle from 1795 to 1834. The obverse of the 2008 Andrew Jackson's Liberty First Spouse coin and medal features the Capped Bust design."], ["MINTING", "All four of these coin were struck in 89.2% silver and 10.8% copper."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "There was also a gold design created by engraver Robert Scot created in 1795, also called the Capped Bust, although it is more popularly known as the \" Turban Head \" because of its unusual, exotic appearance."]], "year": 0},
	"anct_44_bc_eid_mar_denarius_of_brut": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "Ancient Rome"], ["METAL", "silver"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Its symbol is represented in Unicode as 𐆖 (U+10196), a numeral monogram that appeared on the obverse in the Republican period, denoting the 10 asses (\"X\") to 1 denarius (\"I\") conversion rate."], ["THE STORY", "The denarius contained an average 4.5 grams, or 1 ⁄ 72 of a Roman pound, of silver, and was at first tariffed at ten asses, hence its name, which means 'tenner'."], ["MINTING", "A predecessor of the denarius was first struck in 269 or 268 BC, five years before the First Punic War, with an average weight of 6.81 grams, or 1 ⁄ 48 of a Roman pound."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Using the cost of bread as a baseline, this pay equates to around US$20 in 2013 terms. Expressed in terms of the price of silver, and assuming 0.999 purity, a 1 ⁄ 10 troy ounce denarius had a precious metal value of around US$2.60 in 2021."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "From a purity of greater than 90% silver in the 1st century AD, the denarius fell to under 60% purity by 200 AD, and plummeted to 5% purity by 300 AD."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In the New Testament, the gospels refer to the denarius as a day's wage for a common laborer (Matthew 20:2, John 12:5)."]], "year": 0},
	"gold_1907_ultra_high_relief_double": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "$20"], ["DIAMETER", "34 mm"], ["METAL", "copper, gold"], ["DESIGNER", "Augustus Saint-Gaudenz, James B. Longakre"], ["BIG SALE", "$7,590,020"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "A double eagle is a gold coin of the United States with a denomination of $20. The eagle, half eagle, and quarter eagle were defined by name in the Act of Congress originally authorizing them."], ["MINTING", "The early 1907 double eagles and the 1986–1991 gold American eagles are the only instances of Roman numerals denoting the date on American coinage. The period in the \"3.5\" was omitted in error on the first copper coin struck."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the summer of 2002, a 1933 double eagle was auctioned off for US$7,590,020 which shattered the old record of $4,140,000 paid at a public auction for an 1804 silver dollar."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The 1933 double eagle is among the most valuable of U.S. coins, with the sole example currently known to be in private hands–the King Farouk specimen, which was purchased by King Farouk of Egypt in 1944–selling in 2002 for $7,590,020 and resold to an unknown buyer in 2021 for $18.8 million."], ["ONE MORE THING", "With the rising price of gold by June it had climbed to $1339, and by December to $1489."]], "year": 0},
