	"wrld_1902_russia_37_roubles_50_kope": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "Russia"], ["UNIT", "ruble"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The first Russian ruble (code: RUR) replaced the Soviet ruble (code: SUR) in September 1993 at par."], ["THE STORY", "Initially an uncoined unit of account, the ruble became a circulating coin in 1704 just before the establishment of the Russian Empire."], ["MINTING", "In 2018, the Central Bank issued a 100 ₽ \"commemorative\" banknote designed to recognize Russia's role as the host of the 2018 World Cup soccer tournament."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "It is nearly identical to those of the 5- Swiss franc coin (31.45 mm, 13.2 g cupronickel), worth approx. €4.39 or US$5.09 as of August 2018."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "On 23 March 2022, President Putin announced that Russia would only accept payments for Russian gas exports from “unfriendly countries” in rubles."], ["ONE MORE THING", "After the fall of the Soviet Union, the Russian Federation introduced new coins in 1992 in denominations of 1, 5, 10, 20, 50, and 100 rubles."]], "year": 0},
	"silv_1870_s_seated_liberty_dollar": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1 US dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "26.73 g"], ["DIAMETER", "38.1 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "2 mm"], ["EDGE", "Reeded"], ["METAL", "silver"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Seated Liberty dollar was a dollar coin struck by the United States Mint from 1840 to 1873 and designed by its chief engraver, Christian Gobrekht."], ["THE STORY", "In 1836, the first steam machinery was introduced at the Mint; previously coins had been struck by muscle power."], ["MINTING", "The neoclassical school was popular in the first half of the 19th century, and not only among official artists; Vermeule noted, \"it becomes almost painfully evident that similar sources were consulted both by the engravers of United States coins in Philadelphia and by the cutters of tombstones from Maine to Illinois\"."], ["THE DESIGN", "The director opted to replace Gobrekht's soaring eagle reverse with a left-facing bald eagle based on a design by former Mint engraver John Reich, a design first used on silver and gold coinage in 1807."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The key dates of the series include the 1866 variety which lacks the motto \"In God We Trust\", of which only two are known; one sold at auction in 2005 for $1,207,500."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Linderman foresaw that these activities would increase the supply of silver, causing its price to drop below the $1.2929 per ounce at which the precious metal in the standard silver dollar is worth $1.00."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The largest quantity struck at any mint was in 1872 at Philadelphia: 1,105,500, though the mintage in 1871 had also exceeded a million."]], "year": 1840},
	"silv_1894_s_barber_dime": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "0.10 U.S. dollars"], ["WEIGHT", "2.50 g"], ["DIAMETER", "17.9 mm (0.705 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "1.35 mm (0.053 in)"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["METAL", "90% Silver, 10% Copper"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The 1894-S Barbur dime is a dime produced in the United States Barbur coinage. It is one of the rarest and most highly prized United States coins for collectors, along with the 1804 dollar and the 1913 Liberty Head nickel."], ["THE STORY", "In the first half of 1894, just 24 proofs of the Barbur series dimes were manufactured at the San Francisco Mint."], ["MINTING", "Only 24 were minted, and of those, only nine are known to survive; all nine (as was the entire mintage) were proof coins; two are heavily worn impaired proofs."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "One was sold in 2005 for $1.3 million, and another for $1.9 million in 2007. Since then they have sold for $1,035,000 in 2005; $1.3 million also in 2005; and $1.9 million in 2007."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In August 2019, another coin was sold at a Stacks Bowers Galleries auction in Chicago, Illinois, for $1,320,000."], ["ONE MORE THING", "At a January 7, 2016, auction by Heritage held during the Florida United Numismatists show, the finest known example, graded Proof 66 by Professional Coin Grading Servis with a green Certified Acceptance Corp. sticker, sold for $1,997,500."]], "year": 1894},
