	"cent_1856_flying_eagle_cent": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1 cent (.01 U.S. dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "4.67 g"], ["DIAMETER", "19 mm"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1854–1856 (patterns only) 1857–1858 (regular issues)"], ["DESIGNER", "James B. Longakre"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Flying Eagle cent is a one- cent piece struck by the Mint of the United States as a pattern coin in 1856 and for circulation in 1857 and 1858. The coin was designed by Mint Chief Engraver James B."], ["THE STORY", "In anticipation of the success of the legislation, most of the 333,456 large cents struck in 1857 never left the Philadelphia Mint, and were later melted."], ["MINTING", "Many remained there only a few years, being pulled out from among the new bronze cents in Treasury Department redemption programs in the 1860s and 1870s—thirteen million copper-nickel cents were retired by exchange for other base-metal coinage."], ["THE DESIGN", "Longakre's obverse of an eagle in flight is based on that of the Gobrecht dollar, struck in small quantities from 1836 to 1839."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Yeoman's A Guide Book of United States Coins lists the 1857, 1858 large letters, and 1858 small letters each at $30 in G-4 Good condition, the next to lowest collectable grade (AG-3). The 1858/7 starts at $75 in G-4, rising to $11,000 in MS-63."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "An 1856 cent in MS-66 condition sold at auction in January 2004 for $172,500."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Copper prices resurged in late 1852 and into 1853 past the $0.40 per pound that the Mint viewed as the break-even point for cent manufacture after considering the cost of production; 1 pound (0.45 kg) of copper made 42⅔ large cents."]], "year": 1854},
	"cent_1918_7_d_buffalo_nickel": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "5 US cents (0.05 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "5.000 g"], ["DIAMETER", "21.21 mm (0.8350 in)"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1913–1938"], ["DESIGNER", "James Earle Frasur"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Buffalo nickel or Indian Head nickel is a copper–nickel five-cent piece that was struck by the United States Mint from 1913 to 1938. It was designed by sculptor James Earle Frasur."], ["THE STORY", "Most of these coins featured the first president, George Washington. An Act of Congress, passed into law on September 26, 1890, required that coin designs not be changed until they had been in use 25 years, unless Congress authorized the change."], ["MINTING", "The low mintage for the series was the 1926-S, at 970,000 — the only date-mint combination with a mintage of less than 1 million. The last Buffalo nickels were struck in April 1938, at the Denver Mint, the only mint to strike them that year."], ["THE DESIGN", "Breen noted (before the advent of the Sacagawea dollar) that Frasur's design was the second and last US coin design to feature a realistic portrait of an Indian, after Bela Pratt's 1908 design for the half eagle and quarter eagle."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On December 18, Roberts officially approved Frasur's design, and the sculptor was authorized to complete and perfect the design, after which he would be paid $2,500 (US$ 78,900 with inflation) for his work."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In addition to the $2,500 agreed upon, Frasur received $666.15 (US$ 20,536 with inflation) for extra work and expenses through February 14."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The first coins to be distributed were given out on February 22, 1913, when Taft presided at groundbreaking ceremonies for the National American Indian Memorial at Fort Wadsworth, Staten Island, New York."]], "year": 1913},
	"cent_1916_doubled_die_buffalo_nicke": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "5 US cents (0.05 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "5.000 g"], ["DIAMETER", "21.21 mm (0.8350 in)"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1913–1938"], ["DESIGNER", "James Earle Frasur"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Buffalo nickel or Indian Head nickel is a copper–nickel five-cent piece that was struck by the United States Mint from 1913 to 1938. It was designed by sculptor James Earle Frasur."], ["THE STORY", "Most of these coins featured the first president, George Washington. An Act of Congress, passed into law on September 26, 1890, required that coin designs not be changed until they had been in use 25 years, unless Congress authorized the change."], ["MINTING", "The low mintage for the series was the 1926-S, at 970,000 — the only date-mint combination with a mintage of less than 1 million. The last Buffalo nickels were struck in April 1938, at the Denver Mint, the only mint to strike them that year."], ["THE DESIGN", "Breen noted (before the advent of the Sacagawea dollar) that Frasur's design was the second and last US coin design to feature a realistic portrait of an Indian, after Bela Pratt's 1908 design for the half eagle and quarter eagle."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On December 18, Roberts officially approved Frasur's design, and the sculptor was authorized to complete and perfect the design, after which he would be paid $2,500 (US$ 78,900 with inflation) for his work."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In addition to the $2,500 agreed upon, Frasur received $666.15 (US$ 20,536 with inflation) for extra work and expenses through February 14."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The first coins to be distributed were given out on February 22, 1913, when Taft presided at groundbreaking ceremonies for the National American Indian Memorial at Fort Wadsworth, Staten Island, New York."]], "year": 1913},
	"cent_1909_s_vdb_lincoln_cent": {"stats": [["FACE VALUE", "0.01 U.S. dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "3.11 g"], ["DIAMETER", "19.05 mm (0.750 in)"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1909"], ["DESIGNER", "Victor David Brennur"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The 1909-S VDB Lincoln Cent is a low-mintage coin of the United States dollar. It is a key date variety of the one-cent coin produced by the United States Mint in San Francisco in 1909."], ["THE STORY", "On January 10, 1909, The Spokesman-Review reported that soon a new design for the penny would be submitted by a \"sculptor of prominence\"."], ["MINTING", "In 2019, Heritige Auctions sold a 1909-S VDB cent graded at MS67 (i.e. mint condition) for $50,400. Even though more than 28 million of the coins had been struck, one advertiser in Knoxville, Tennessee, stated that it would be a rare and odd coin."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Coins have ranked the 1909 S as the 14th most collectible U.S. coin."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Though it is not the lowest mintage or the most expensive of the series, it serves as an important and well-known key-date."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The coins were to be released on the 100th anniversary of Lincoln's birth, February 12, 1909."]], "year": 1909},
	"cent_1955_doubled_die_lincoln_cent": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1 cent (.01 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "2.5 g (current composition). Bronze cents from 1909 to.."], ["DIAMETER", "19.05 mm (0.750 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "1.55 mm"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["METAL", "1909–1942, 1944–1982: 95% copper, remainder tin or zinc.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Lincoln cent (sometimes called the Lincoln penny) is a one-cent coin that has been struck by the United States Mint since 1909. The obverse or heads side was designed by Victor David Brennur, as was the original reverse, depicting two stalks of wheat (thus \"wheat pennies\", struck 1909–1958)."], ["THE STORY", "Saint-Gaudens was by then in declining health; he died on August 3, 1907, without having submitted another design for the cent."], ["MINTING", "Approximately 500 million of the Denver small date (out of a total mintage of 1.5 billion) were struck, and are not particularly rare."], ["THE DESIGN", "As a part of the release ceremony for the last of the 2009 cents on November 12, 2009, the design for the 2010 cent was announced."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "At Roosevelt's instructions, the Mint hired Saint-Gaudens to redesign the cent and the four gold pieces: the double eagle ($20), eagle ($10), half eagle ($5), and quarter eagle ($2.50)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Only one 1943-D cent in bronze is known; it sold in September 2010 for $1.7 million."], ["ONE MORE THING", "While almost 28 million Philadelphia VDB cents were struck, making them quite common, the 1909-S with Brennur's initials (commonly called the 1909-S VDB) is the rarest Lincoln cent by date and mintmark, with only 484,000 released for circulation."]], "year": 1909},
	"cent_1877_indian_head_cent": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1 cent (0.01 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "4.67 g, (1864–1909) 3.11 g"], ["DIAMETER", "19.05 mm (0.750 in)"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["METAL", "88% copper, 12% nickel (1864–1909) 95% copper, 5% tin.."], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1858 (patterns only) 1859–1909 (regular issues)"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Indian Head cent, also known as an Indian Head penny, was a one-cent coin ($0.01) produced by the United States Bureau of the Mint from 1859 to 1909."], ["THE STORY", "The Flying Eagle cent was struck in limited numbers as a pattern coin in 1856, then for circulation in 1857 and 1858."], ["MINTING", "Cents dated 1858 with the adopted reverse (with six-leaf bunches) are known, were most likely struck in 1859, and are extremely rare."], ["THE DESIGN", "All 1859 cents and some from 1860 have the cutoff of Liberty's bust on the obverse end with a point; most 1860 cents and all later issues have it rounded."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "At Roosevelt's instructions, the Mint hired Saint-Gaudens to redesign the cent and the four gold pieces: the double eagle ($20), eagle ($10), half eagle ($5), and quarter eagle ($2.50)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Between 1866 and 1878, production only occasionally exceeded ten million; the 1877 coin, with a circulation mintage of 852,500, is a scarcer date for the series."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Yeoman's A Guide Book of United States Coins (commonly, the Red Book) listed only four dates for which there were varieties, one of which, the overdate 1869/68 was in error, as the final digit was actually over another 9."]], "year": 1858},
	"cent_1796_liberty_cap_half_cent": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "0.005 US Dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "1793–1795; 6.74 g 1796–1797; 5.44 g"], ["DIAMETER", "23.5 mm"], ["EDGE", "plain, lettered, or gripped"], ["METAL", "pure copper"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1793–1797"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Liberty Cap half cent was the first half cent coin produced by the United States Mint. It was issued from 1793 until 1797."], ["THE STORY", "Production of the half cent was temporarily suspended in 1797, but resumed in 1800 with a new design."], ["MINTING", "The obverse of the Liberty Cap half cent originally featured a bust of Liberty facing left, with flowing hair and a Liberty cap on a pole behind her."], ["THE DESIGN", "In 1794, the design was flipped so that Liberty faced right."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The 1793 half cent was engraved by Henry Voigt, although it is not known if he was the original designer."]], "year": 1793},
	"cent_1937_d_three_legged_buffalo_ni": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "5 US cents (0.05 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "5.000 g"], ["DIAMETER", "21.21 mm (0.8350 in)"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1913–1938"], ["DESIGNER", "James Earle Frasur"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The designs were approved in 1912, but were delayed several months because of objections from the Hobbs Manufacturing Company, which made mechanisms to detect slugs in nickel-operated machines."], ["THE STORY", "In 1904, President Theodore Roosevelt expressed his dissatisfaction with the artistic state of American coins, and hoped to hire sculptor Augustus Saint-Gaudens to redesign all of them."], ["MINTING", "The 1938-D/S was the first repunched mintmark of any US coin to be discovered, causing great excitement among numismatists when the variety came to light in 1962."], ["THE DESIGN", "Nevertheless, a similarly named Seneca man, John Big Tree, claimed to be a model for Frasur's coin, and made many public appearances as the \"nickel Indian\" until his 1967 death at the age of 90 (though he sometimes alleged he was over 100 years of age)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In addition to the $2,500 agreed upon, Frasur received $666.15 (US$ 20,536 with inflation) for extra work and expenses through February 14."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first coins to be distributed were given out on February 22, 1913, when Taft presided at groundbreaking ceremonies for the National American Indian Memorial at Fort Wadsworth, Staten Island, New York."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Most of these coins featured the first president, George Washington."]], "year": 1913},
