	"cent_1926_s_buffalo_nickel": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "5 US cents (0.05 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "5.000 g"], ["DIAMETER", "21.21 mm (0.8350 in)"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1913–1938"], ["DESIGNER", "James Earle Frasur"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Buffalo nickel or Indian Head nickel is a copper–nickel five-cent piece that was struck by the United States Mint from 1913 to 1938. It was designed by sculptor James Earle Frasur."], ["THE STORY", "Most of these coins featured the first president, George Washington. An Act of Congress, passed into law on September 26, 1890, required that coin designs not be changed until they had been in use 25 years, unless Congress authorized the change."], ["MINTING", "The low mintage for the series was the 1926-S, at 970,000 — the only date-mint combination with a mintage of less than 1 million. The last Buffalo nickels were struck in April 1938, at the Denver Mint, the only mint to strike them that year."], ["THE DESIGN", "Breen noted (before the advent of the Sacagawea dollar) that Frasur's design was the second and last US coin design to feature a realistic portrait of an Indian, after Bela Pratt's 1908 design for the half eagle and quarter eagle."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On December 18, Roberts officially approved Frasur's design, and the sculptor was authorized to complete and perfect the design, after which he would be paid $2,500 (US$ 78,900 with inflation) for his work."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In addition to the $2,500 agreed upon, Frasur received $666.15 (US$ 20,536 with inflation) for extra work and expenses through February 14."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The first coins to be distributed were given out on February 22, 1913, when Taft presided at groundbreaking ceremonies for the National American Indian Memorial at Fort Wadsworth, Staten Island, New York."]], "year": 1913},
	"cent_1867_shield_nickel_with_rays_p": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "5 cents (0.05 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "5.000 g (0.1615 troy oz)"], ["DIAMETER", "20.50 mm (0.8077 in)"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["METAL", "cupronickel"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1866–1883"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "In 1864, the Mint successfully introduced low-denomination coins, whose intrinsic worth did not approach their face value."], ["THE STORY", "On March 3, 1865, Congress passed legislation authorizing the Mint to strike three-cent pieces of 75% copper and 25% nickel. The alloy used was originally.892 silver with the remainder copper; in 1837 the silver portion was increased to.900."], ["MINTING", "Shield nickel proof mintages from before 1878 are modern estimates and may vary—for example, Bowers estimates 800–1,200 for the 1866 piece, while Peters estimates 375+."], ["THE DESIGN", "Taxay suggested that Linderman was most likely influenced by Wharton and the metal's other advocates."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 1871, Congress alleviated the problem by passing legislation allowing the Treasury to redeem unlimited quantities of nickels and other low-denomination coins when presented in lots of not less than $20."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The 1880 nickel, with only 16,000 pieces struck for circulation, remains the rarest non-proof Shield nickel today."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Proof Shield nickels continued to be struck until June 26, 1883 when the last 1500 proof shield nickels were produced."]], "year": 1866},
	"cent_1864_two_cent_piece_small_mott": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["WEIGHT", "6.22 g"], ["DIAMETER", "23.00 mm"], ["EDGE", "plain"], ["METAL", "copper-based alloy"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1863 (patterns only) 1864–1873 (regular issues)"], ["DESIGNER", "James B. Longakre"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The two-cent piece was produced by the Mint of the United States for circulation from 1864 to 1872 and for collectors in 1873. Longakre, there were decreasing mintages each year, as other minor coins such as the nickel proved more popular."], ["THE STORY", "The legislation passed the House, and the Coinage Act of 1864 was signed by President Abraham Lincoln on April 22, 1864."], ["MINTING", "A few thousand of the first circulation strikes, as well as a handful of proof coins, came from a prototype die with smaller letters in the motto than all other 1864 pieces."], ["THE DESIGN", "Art historian Cornelius Vermeule deemed the two-cent piece \"the most Gothic and the most expressive of the Civil War\" of all American coins."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Yeoman lists the 1864 small motto in Proof condition, at $20,000 and in Very Fine (VF-20) it is $600."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Boutwell asked Congress to pass a redemption act, and it did so on March 3, 1871, allowing for the redemption of minor coinage in lots of not less than $20."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Silver coins still did not circulate in much of the nation, and the new coins (joined by the three- and five-cent pieces of copper-nickel, first struck in 1865 and 1866 respectively) answered the need for small change."]], "year": 1863},
	"cent_1873_two_cent_piece_proof": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["WEIGHT", "6.22 g"], ["DIAMETER", "23.00 mm"], ["EDGE", "plain"], ["METAL", "copper-based alloy"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1863 (patterns only) 1864–1873 (regular issues)"], ["DESIGNER", "James B. Longakre"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The two-cent piece was produced by the Mint of the United States for circulation from 1864 to 1872 and for collectors in 1873. Longakre, there were decreasing mintages each year, as other minor coins such as the nickel proved more popular."], ["THE STORY", "The legislation passed the House, and the Coinage Act of 1864 was signed by President Abraham Lincoln on April 22, 1864."], ["MINTING", "A few thousand of the first circulation strikes, as well as a handful of proof coins, came from a prototype die with smaller letters in the motto than all other 1864 pieces."], ["THE DESIGN", "Art historian Cornelius Vermeule deemed the two-cent piece \"the most Gothic and the most expressive of the Civil War\" of all American coins."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Yeoman lists the 1864 small motto in Proof condition, at $20,000 and in Very Fine (VF-20) it is $600."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Boutwell asked Congress to pass a redemption act, and it did so on March 3, 1871, allowing for the redemption of minor coinage in lots of not less than $20."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Silver coins still did not circulate in much of the nation, and the new coins (joined by the three- and five-cent pieces of copper-nickel, first struck in 1865 and 1866 respectively) answered the need for small change."]], "year": 1863},
	"cent_1877_three_cent_nickel_proof": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "3 cents (0.03 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "1.94 g"], ["DIAMETER", "17.9 mm"], ["EDGE", "plain"], ["METAL", "nickel, copper"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1865–1889"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The copper-nickel three-cent piece, often called a three-cent nickel piece or three-cent nickel, was designed by US Mint Chief Engraver James B. Longakre and struck by the United States Bureau of the Mint from 1865 to 1889."], ["THE STORY", "Dickinson's bill passed on March 3, 1851, and in addition to authorizing the new three-cent silver, lowered rates for most domestic mails. The large cent was replaced by a smaller version made of 88% copper and 12% nickel in 1857."], ["MINTING", "Although the Liberty as used on the three-cent nickel piece is closest to Longakre's experimental cents of 1857 and quarter eagles of 1860, she resembles most of the Chief Engraver's other depictions of Liberty."], ["THE DESIGN", "Longakre had, since 1849, designed coins with various visages of the goddess Liberty, based on a bust, Venus Accroupie, he had seen in a Philadelphia museum on loan from the Vatican."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Yeoman's A Guide Book of United States Coins, only the pieces from 1882 to 1887 catalog for more than $100 in worn Good-4 condition; common dates list for $15 to $20 in that condition."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Although more than a million were minted in 1881, another blow to the three-cent piece occurred on October 1, 1883, when first-class mail rates were lowered from three to two cents for the first 0.5 ounces (14 g)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The highest value listed is for the 1877, struck only in proof with a mintage of 900, at $2,000."]], "year": 1865},
	"cent_1885_liberty_head_nickel": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "5 cents (.05 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "5.000 g (0.1615 troy oz)"], ["DIAMETER", "21.21 mm (0.8350 in)"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["METAL", "cupronickel"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1883–1913"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Liberty Head nickel, sometimes referred to as the V nickel because of its reverse (or tails) design, is an American five-cent piece. It was struck for circulation from 1883 until 1912, with at least five pieces being surreptitiously struck dated 1913."], ["THE STORY", "The nickel would retain its weight of 5 grams (0.18 oz), but its diameter would be increased to 22 millimetres (0.87 in)."], ["MINTING", "Phelan to pay the first fare on the city's first streetcar on December 28, 1912. In 1896, pattern nickels were struck for the first time since 1885, when experimental, holed coins had been tested."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Recent sales of a 1913 Liberty Head nickel were in April 2013 for more than $3.1 million and for $4.5 million at auction in August 2018. Excluding the 1913 nickel, the 1912-S, with only 238,000 struck, is by far the rarest in the series."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "One price recorded for a 1913 Liberty Head nickel was in January 2010, when one sold for $3,737,500 in an auction."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Striking of the new coins began on January 30, 1883, and the Mint placed the first pieces in circulation on February 1."]], "year": 1883},
