	"cent_1969_s_doubled_die_lincoln_cen": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1 cent (.01 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "2.5 g (current composition). Bronze cents from 1909 to.."], ["DIAMETER", "19.05 mm (0.750 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "1.55 mm"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["METAL", "1909–1942, 1944–1982: 95% copper, remainder tin or zinc.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Lincoln cent (sometimes called the Lincoln penny) is a one-cent coin that has been struck by the United States Mint since 1909. The obverse or heads side was designed by Victor David Brennur, as was the original reverse, depicting two stalks of wheat (thus \"wheat pennies\", struck 1909–1958)."], ["THE STORY", "Saint-Gaudens was by then in declining health; he died on August 3, 1907, without having submitted another design for the cent."], ["MINTING", "Approximately 500 million of the Denver small date (out of a total mintage of 1.5 billion) were struck, and are not particularly rare."], ["THE DESIGN", "As a part of the release ceremony for the last of the 2009 cents on November 12, 2009, the design for the 2010 cent was announced."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "At Roosevelt's instructions, the Mint hired Saint-Gaudens to redesign the cent and the four gold pieces: the double eagle ($20), eagle ($10), half eagle ($5), and quarter eagle ($2.50)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Only one 1943-D cent in bronze is known; it sold in September 2010 for $1.7 million."], ["ONE MORE THING", "While almost 28 million Philadelphia VDB cents were struck, making them quite common, the 1909-S with Brennur's initials (commonly called the 1909-S VDB) is the rarest Lincoln cent by date and mintmark, with only 484,000 released for circulation."]], "year": 1909},
