	"cent_1865_three_cent_nickel": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "3 cents (0.03 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "1.94 g"], ["DIAMETER", "17.9 mm"], ["EDGE", "plain"], ["METAL", "nickel, copper"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1865–1889"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The copper-nickel three-cent piece, often called a three-cent nickel piece or three-cent nickel, was designed by US Mint Chief Engraver James B. Longakre and struck by the United States Bureau of the Mint from 1865 to 1889."], ["THE STORY", "Dickinson's bill passed on March 3, 1851, and in addition to authorizing the new three-cent silver, lowered rates for most domestic mails. The large cent was replaced by a smaller version made of 88% copper and 12% nickel in 1857."], ["MINTING", "Although the Liberty as used on the three-cent nickel piece is closest to Longakre's experimental cents of 1857 and quarter eagles of 1860, she resembles most of the Chief Engraver's other depictions of Liberty."], ["THE DESIGN", "Longakre had, since 1849, designed coins with various visages of the goddess Liberty, based on a bust, Venus Accroupie, he had seen in a Philadelphia museum on loan from the Vatican."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Yeoman's A Guide Book of United States Coins, only the pieces from 1882 to 1887 catalog for more than $100 in worn Good-4 condition; common dates list for $15 to $20 in that condition."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Although more than a million were minted in 1881, another blow to the three-cent piece occurred on October 1, 1883, when first-class mail rates were lowered from three to two cents for the first 0.5 ounces (14 g)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The highest value listed is for the 1877, struck only in proof with a mintage of 900, at $2,000."]], "year": 1865},
	"cent_1880_shield_nickel": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "5 cents (0.05 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "5.000 g (0.1615 troy oz)"], ["DIAMETER", "20.50 mm (0.8077 in)"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["METAL", "cupronickel"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1866–1883"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Shield nickel was the first United States five-cent piece to be made out of copper-nickel, the same alloy of which American nickels are struck today. Longakre, the coin was issued from 1866 until 1883, when it was replaced by the Liberty Head nickel."], ["THE STORY", "The alloy used was originally.892 silver with the remainder copper; in 1837 the silver portion was increased to.900. On March 3, 1865, Congress passed legislation authorizing the Mint to strike three-cent pieces of 75% copper and 25% nickel."], ["MINTING", "As the Treasury had a large stock of nickels in storage, only small numbers were struck over the next few years; full-scale production began again on December 12, 1881."], ["THE DESIGN", "The transition to the new design was to occur on February 1, 1867, but it is likely the mint used up the remaining dies with the old design in the interest of economy."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The 1880 nickel, with only 16,000 pieces struck for circulation, remains the rarest non-proof Shield nickel today. In 1864, the Mint successfully introduced low-denomination coins, whose intrinsic worth did not approach their face value."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Proof Shield nickels continued to be struck until June 26, 1883 when the last 1500 proof shield nickels were produced."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 1871, Congress alleviated the problem by passing legislation allowing the Treasury to redeem unlimited quantities of nickels and other low-denomination coins when presented in lots of not less than $20."]], "year": 1866},
	"cent_1983_doubled_die_reverse_linco": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1 cent (.01 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "2.5 g (current composition). Bronze cents from 1909 to.."], ["DIAMETER", "19.05 mm (0.750 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "1.55 mm"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["METAL", "1909–1942, 1944–1982: 95% copper, remainder tin or zinc.."]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The coin has seen several reverse, or tails, designs and now bears one by Lyndall Basz depicting a Union shield."], ["THE STORY", "While the contents of their conversations were never recorded, it appears they discussed Roosevelt's plans for coinage redesign."], ["MINTING", "Approximately 500 million of the Denver small date (out of a total mintage of 1.5 billion) were struck, and are not particularly rare."], ["THE DESIGN", "Prices for the small date coins, of which approximately two million had been struck at Philadelphia, continued to increase until 1964, when the bubble burst."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In September 2010, a record was set when a unique off-metal 1943-D Copper Cent sold for $1.7 million. Only one 1943-D cent in bronze is known; it sold in September 2010 for $1.7 million."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Presidential $1 Coin Act of 2005 required that the cent's reverse be redesigned for 2009, and that four designs be issued to celebrate the Abraham Lincoln Bicentennial."], ["ONE MORE THING", "As a part of the release ceremony for the last of the 2009 cents on November 12, 2009, the design for the 2010 cent was announced."]], "year": 1909},
