	"cent_1857_flying_eagle_cent": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1 cent (.01 U.S. dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "4.67 g"], ["DIAMETER", "19 mm"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1854–1856 (patterns only) 1857–1858 (regular issues)"], ["DESIGNER", "James B. Longakre"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Flying Eagle cent is a one- cent piece struck by the Mint of the United States as a pattern coin in 1856 and for circulation in 1857 and 1858. The coin was designed by Mint Chief Engraver James B."], ["THE STORY", "In anticipation of the success of the legislation, most of the 333,456 large cents struck in 1857 never left the Philadelphia Mint, and were later melted."], ["MINTING", "Many remained there only a few years, being pulled out from among the new bronze cents in Treasury Department redemption programs in the 1860s and 1870s—thirteen million copper-nickel cents were retired by exchange for other base-metal coinage."], ["THE DESIGN", "Longakre's obverse of an eagle in flight is based on that of the Gobrecht dollar, struck in small quantities from 1836 to 1839."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Yeoman's A Guide Book of United States Coins lists the 1857, 1858 large letters, and 1858 small letters each at $30 in G-4 Good condition, the next to lowest collectable grade (AG-3). The 1858/7 starts at $75 in G-4, rising to $11,000 in MS-63."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "An 1856 cent in MS-66 condition sold at auction in January 2004 for $172,500."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Copper prices resurged in late 1852 and into 1853 past the $0.40 per pound that the Mint viewed as the break-even point for cent manufacture after considering the cost of production; 1 pound (0.45 kg) of copper made 42⅔ large cents."]], "year": 1854},
	"cent_1943_steel_lincoln_cent": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "$0.01 U.S. dollars"], ["WEIGHT", "2.702 g"], ["DIAMETER", "19.05 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "1.55 mm"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["METAL", "99% steel with a thin layer of zinc"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Philadelphia, Denver, and San Francisco mints each produced these 1943 Lincoln cents."], ["THE STORY", "Although they continued to circulate into the 1960s, the mint collected large numbers of the 1943 cents and destroyed them."], ["MINTING", "That example appeared in a 1958 Abe Kosoff sale, but was withdrawn prior to the sale; one mint condition Denver Mint specimen sold for over $1.7 million in 2010."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 1943, the cost of a gross ton of steel was $34."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In an error similar to the 1943 cents, a few 1944 cents were struck on steel planchets left over from 1943."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Another explanation credits the error to the production of 25 million Belgian two franc pieces by the Philadelphia mint after that country's liberation from the Nazis."]], "year": 1943},
	"cent_1936_buffalo_nickel": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "5 US cents (0.05 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "5.000 g"], ["DIAMETER", "21.21 mm (0.8350 in)"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1913–1938"], ["DESIGNER", "James Earle Frasur"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Buffalo nickel or Indian Head nickel is a copper–nickel five-cent piece that was struck by the United States Mint from 1913 to 1938. It was designed by sculptor James Earle Frasur."], ["THE STORY", "Most of these coins featured the first president, George Washington. An Act of Congress, passed into law on September 26, 1890, required that coin designs not be changed until they had been in use 25 years, unless Congress authorized the change."], ["MINTING", "The low mintage for the series was the 1926-S, at 970,000 — the only date-mint combination with a mintage of less than 1 million. The last Buffalo nickels were struck in April 1938, at the Denver Mint, the only mint to strike them that year."], ["THE DESIGN", "Breen noted (before the advent of the Sacagawea dollar) that Frasur's design was the second and last US coin design to feature a realistic portrait of an Indian, after Bela Pratt's 1908 design for the half eagle and quarter eagle."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On December 18, Roberts officially approved Frasur's design, and the sculptor was authorized to complete and perfect the design, after which he would be paid $2,500 (US$ 78,900 with inflation) for his work."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In addition to the $2,500 agreed upon, Frasur received $666.15 (US$ 20,536 with inflation) for extra work and expenses through February 14."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The first coins to be distributed were given out on February 22, 1913, when Taft presided at groundbreaking ceremonies for the National American Indian Memorial at Fort Wadsworth, Staten Island, New York."]], "year": 1913},
	"cent_1944_wheat_penny": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1 cent (.01 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "2.5 g (current composition). Bronze cents from 1909 to.."], ["DIAMETER", "19.05 mm (0.750 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "1.55 mm"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["METAL", "1909–1942, 1944–1982: 95% copper, remainder tin or zinc.."]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "In 1905, sculptor Augustus Saint-Gaudens was hired by the Mint to redesign the cent and the four gold coins, which did not require congressional approval."], ["THE STORY", "While the contents of their conversations were never recorded, it appears they discussed Roosevelt's plans for coinage redesign."], ["MINTING", "Approximately 500 million of the Denver small date (out of a total mintage of 1.5 billion) were struck, and are not particularly rare."], ["THE DESIGN", "On Sunday morning, December 21, 1958, President Eisenhower's press secretary, James Hagerty, issued a press release announcing that a new reverse design for the cent would begin production on January 2, 1959."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "While almost 28 million Philadelphia VDB cents were struck, making them quite common, the 1909-S with Brennur's initials (commonly called the 1909-S VDB) is the rarest Lincoln cent by date and mintmark, with only 484,000 released for circulation."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In September 2010, a record was set when a unique off-metal 1943-D Copper Cent sold for $1.7 million."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Sealed bags of 1960 cents, with a face value of $50, sold for as much as $12,000."]], "year": 1909},
