	"silv_1838_o_capped_bust_half_dollar": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "0.50 U.S. dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "11.340 g (0.365 troy oz)"], ["DIAMETER", "30.61 mm (1.205 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "2.15 mm (0.085 in)"], ["EDGE", "150 reeds"], ["METAL", "1964: 90% Ag 10% Cu; 1965–1970: 60% Cu 40% Ag.."]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The half dollar, sometimes referred to as the half for short or 50-cent piece, is a United States coin worth 50 cents, or one half of a dollar."], ["THE STORY", "On December 1, 1794, the first half dollars, approximately 5,300 pieces, were delivered. Another 18,000 were produced in January 1795 using dies of 1794, to save the expense of making new ones."], ["MINTING", "Rufus Tyler, chief coiner of the New Orleans mint, wrote to Mint Director Patterson of the problem on February 25, 1839. The sole surviving Orleans minted 1838 is one of the rarest of all American coins."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The 1838 Philadelphia-produced half dollars are extremely rare, with two separate specimens having sold for $632,500 in Heritage auctions in 2005 and 2008 respectively."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "From 2001-2020, half dollars were minted only for collectors, due to large Federal Reserve and government inventories of pre-2001 coins; this was primarily due to a lack of demand and large quantity returns of halves from casinos that switched to using \"coin-less\" slot machines."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The silver content of dimes and quarters was eliminated, but the Kennedy half-dollar, introduced in 1964, contained silver (reduced from 90% in 1964 to 40% from 1965 to 1970)."]], "year": 1794},
	"silv_1797_draped_bust_half_dollar": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1.00 U.S. dollars"], ["WEIGHT", "26.96 g"], ["DIAMETER", "39–40 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "2 mm"], ["EDGE", "Lettered"], ["METAL", "copper, silver"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Draped Bust dollar is a United States dollar coin minted from 1795 to 1803, and was reproduced, dated 1804, into the 1850s. The design succeeded the Flowing Hair dollar, which began mintage in 1794 and was the first silver dollar struck by the United States Mint."], ["THE STORY", "The Coinage Act of 1792 called for the silver coinage to be struck in an alloy consisting of 89.2% silver and 10.8% copper."], ["MINTING", "Bullion deposits continued to decline, and in 1797, silver dollar production reached the lowest point since 1794 with a mintage of just 7,776 pieces."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 1867, one of the original 1804 dollars was sold at auction for $750 ($ 16,350 today). Seven years later, on November 27, 1874, a specimen sold for $700 ($ 18,851 today)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The coins continued to gain popularity throughout the 20th century, and the price reached an all-time high in 1999, when an example graded Proof-68 was sold at auction for $4,140,000."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In November 1797, the Bank deposited approximately $30,000 worth of French silver."]], "year": 1795},
	"silv_1873_cc_no_arrows_seated_dime": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "0.10 U.S. dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "2.268 g (0.0729 troy oz)"], ["DIAMETER", "17.91 mm (0.705 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "1.35 mm (0.053 in)"], ["EDGE", "118 reeds"], ["METAL", "Current—91.67% copper, 8.33% nickel Prior to 1965—90%.."]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The dime is the smallest in diameter and is the thinnest of all U.S. coins currently minted for circulation, being 0.705 inches (17.91 millimeters) in diameter and 0.053 in (1.35 mm) in thickness."], ["THE STORY", "The first known proposal for a decimal -based coinage system in the United States was made in 1783 by Thomas Jefferson, Benjamin Franklin, Alexander Hamilton, and David Rittenhouse."], ["MINTING", "Since then, the \"P\" mint mark 2015 reverse proof dime and \"W\" mint mark 2015 proof dime, minted at Philadelphia and West Point for inclusion in the March of Dimes collector set, have the lowest mintages with 75,000 pieces struck for each."], ["THE DESIGN", "Since its introduction in 1796, the dime has been issued in six different major types, excluding the 1792 \"disme\"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "From 1796 to 1837, dimes were composed of 89.24% silver and 10.76% copper, the value of which required the coins to be physically very small to prevent their commodity value from being worth more than face value."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "None were used in 1965–67, and Philadelphia did not show a mintmark until 1980 (in 1982, an error left the \"P\" off a small number of dimes, which are now valuable)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 1792, a limited number of dismes were minted but never circulated."]], "year": 1796},
	"silv_1876_cc_twenty_cent_piece": {"stats": [["FACE VALUE", "20 cents (0.20 US dollar)"], ["WEIGHT", "5 g"], ["DIAMETER", "22 mm"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["SILVER", "0.1447 troy oz"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1875–1878"], ["DESIGNER", "Christian Gobrekht"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "After the first year, in which over a million were minted, there was little demand, and the denomination was abolished in 1878."], ["THE STORY", "On March 31, 1875, after the enactment of Jones's bill, Pollock sent Linderman additional patterns, all by Barber, and even more on April 12."], ["MINTING", "That design, by the late chief engraver, Christian Gobrekht, following a concept by Thomas Sully and Titian Peale, was first used in 1836 and by 1840 was on all denominations of silver coins then being struck."], ["THE DESIGN", "In August 1874, Philadelphia Mint superintendent James Pollock sent him patterns with an obverse showing a seated Liberty by Philadelphia sculptor Joseph A."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Fewer than two dozen are known; one sold at auction for $870,000 in 2022. Yeoman's A Guide Book of United States Coins (the Red Book), is the 1875-S, listed at $110 in good-4 condition."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "At 22 millimetres (0.87 in), the twenty-cent piece was only slightly smaller than the quarter at 24.3 millimetres (0.96 in), and the two pieces had near-identical obverses."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The mintage for 1876 was low (coining took place at Philadelphia and Carson City), and only proof specimens were coined in 1877 and 1878, at Philadelphia."]], "year": 1875},
	"silv_1792_half_disme": {"stats": [["FIRST OUT", "1792"], ["BIG SALE", "$138,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The 1792 half disme is an American silver coin with a face value of five cents (1 ⁄ 20 U.S. dollar) which was minted in 1792."], ["THE STORY", "To the contrary, evidence uncovered from Thomas Jefferson's memorandum book shows that he provided \"75 (Mexican) Silver Dollars\" and received the first 1,500 half dismes."], ["MINTING", "Approximately 10% of these survive today; one expert estimated between 250 and 400 half dismes exist, and most appear to have been used in circulation for some time."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The highest numerically graded piece, an NGZ MS68, sold for $1,500,000 by private treaty transaction in 2007. An about uncirculated (AU55 on the Sheldon coin grading scale) 1792 half disme was auctioned for $138,000 on July 24, 2004."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "A 1792 half disme graded MS65 was valued in 2018 on the American reality television series Pawn Stars at $500,000–600,000."], ["ONE MORE THING", "A specimen strike from the Starr collection, graded MS67 by PCGZ, sold for $1,322,500 on April 26, 2006."]], "year": 1792},
	"silv_1893_s_morgan_dollar": {"stats": [["FACE VALUE", "One dollar"], ["EDGE", "Reeding"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1893 –1893"], ["DESIGNER", "George T. Moorgan"], ["MINT MARKS", "San Francisco"], ["BIG SALE", "$646,250"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The 1893-S Moorgan dollar is a United States dollar coin struck in 1893 at the San Francisco Mint. It is the lowest mintage business strike Moorgan dollar in the series."], ["THE STORY", "Later the 1918 Pittman Act called for many silver dollars to be melted."], ["MINTING", "Knowledgeable collectors can detect a fake because all 1893-S Moorgans struck using the same obverse die, and only two different reverse dies."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 2014 the Eliasberg 1893-S Moorgan dollar sold for US$646,250 at auction. In 2021, a PCGZ graded An 1893-S Moorgan dollar in MS -67 sold for US$2,086,875.00."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In March 2023 an 1893-S graded by Numismatic Guaranty Company (NGZ), in a MS-64, grade sold for US$372,000."]], "year": 1893},
	"silv_1895_morgan_dollar_proof": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1 United States dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "26.73 g (412 1 ⁄ 2 gr)"], ["DIAMETER", "38.1 mm (1.5 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "2.4 mm (0.09 in)"], ["EDGE", "Reeded"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1878–1904, 1921, 2021–present"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Moorgan dollar is a United States dollar coin minted from 1878 to 1904, in 1921, and beginning again in 2021 as a collectible."], ["THE STORY", "In total, Moorgan had five sittings with Williams; he declared her profile to be the most perfect he had seen."], ["MINTING", "In 1921 production was resumed for one year only, with this year being the only one where the Denver mint was used, until 2021. The first acceptable strike, after adjustments to the press, was coined at 3:17 p.m. at the Philadelphia Mint."], ["THE DESIGN", "In total, 100,000 gold and 500,000 silver commemorative coins were authorized."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 2021 an 1893-S Moorgan dollar in MS-67 sold for US$2,086,875.00. In total, the sales generated $107 million in revenue."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The 2023 Uncirculated Moorgan Dollar was released by the US Mint on July 13, 2023, and sold out on the first day of issue."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The U.S. only minted the Moorgan dollar again during 1921, the only year in which Moorgan dollars were struck at the Denver mint."]], "year": 1878},
	"silv_1836_gobrecht_dollar": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1 U.S. dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "26.73–26.92 g"], ["DIAMETER", "38.1 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "2 mm"], ["EDGE", "Reeded"], ["METAL", "silver"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Gobrekht dollar, minted from 1836 to 1839, was the first silver dollar struck for circulation by the United States Mint after production of that denomination had been halted in 1806 (the last previous silver dollars were struck in 1804 but dated 1803)."], ["MINTING", "When the full-scale production began, the reverse eagle was surrounded by 26 stars rather than 24 as Patterson had originally requested of Sullie, as the states of Arkansas and Michigan had been admitted to the Union since Patterson's letter was penned in 1835."], ["THE DESIGN", "Shortly thereafter, Director Patterson approached two well-known Philadelphia artists, Titian Peale and Thomas Sullie, to create a design that would be used to overhaul most of the American coins then in production."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "An act of January 18, 1837, officially changed the legal standard for silver coins from 89.2% to 90% silver."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In total, 1,000 pieces dated 1836 were struck in 89.2% silver and 600 in 90% silver."]], "year": 1836},
	"silv_1796_draped_bust_quarter": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "\" Draped Bust \" was the name given to a design of United States coins. It appeared on much of the regular-issue copper and silver United States coinage, 1796–1807."], ["MINTING", "In 1796, Congress responded to the almost universal dissatisfaction of the first coins (Flowing Hair dollar) and decreed a new design."], ["THE DESIGN", "The famous 1804 silver dollar has this design as well the reverse of the Kennedy half dollar in 1964."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The first, for copper coins, features the value of the coin (half cent or one cent) surrounded by a wreath or vine."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "By comparison, the Tennessee State Quarter, one of five struck in 2002, had a mintage of 650,000,000 (the mintage of all state quarters in 2002 was over 3 billion)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Only 3,000 were minted and the vast majority of these were either lost, melted or wore out."]], "year": 0},
	"silv_1827_capped_bust_quarter_proof": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The Capped Bust coinage of the United States consisted of a half dime, dime, quarter and half dollar."], ["THE STORY", "The obverse of the 2008 Andrew Jackson's Liberty First Spouse coin and medal features the Capped Bust design."], ["MINTING", "All four of these coin were struck in 89.2% silver and 10.8% copper."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "There was also a gold design created by engraver Robert Scot created in 1795, also called the Capped Bust, although it is more popularly known as the \" Turban Head \" because of its unusual, exotic appearance."]], "year": 0},
	"silv_1873_cc_seated_liberty_quarter": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "0.25 U.S. Dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "0.2 oz. (5.67 g)"], ["DIAMETER", "0.955 in. (24.257 mm)"], ["THICKNESS", "0.069 in. (1.7526 mm)"], ["EDGE", "119 reeds"], ["METAL", "From 1965: 91.67% Cu, 8.33% Ni 1932–1964: 0.2204 oz.."]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "With the cupronickel layers comprising 1/3 of total weight, the coin's overall composition is therefore 8.33% nickel, 91.67% copper."], ["THE STORY", "On December 25, 2020, the Mint announced the successful design, by Benjamin Sowards as sculpted by Michael Gaudioso."], ["MINTING", "The choice of a quarter-dollar as a denomination, as opposed to the 1 ⁄ 5 or the 20-cent piece that is more common elsewhere, originated with the practice of dividing Spanish milled dollars into eight wedge-shaped segments, which gave rise to the name \"piece of eight\" for that coin."], ["THE DESIGN", "From 2027 to 2030, the Mint may produce up to five coins each year featuring youth sports."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "For the first three years of clad production, in lieu of proof sets, specimen sets were specially sold as \"Special Mint Sets\" minted at the San Francisco mint in 1965, 1966, and 1967 (Deep Cameo versions of these coins are highly valued because of their rarity)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The copper-nickel clad Washington quarter was first issued in 1965 and as part of the switch, the Philadelphia mintmark was added in 1968, which did not reappear on any US coin denomination until 1968."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 1965–1967 cupro-nickel coins bore no mint mark; quarters minted in 1968–1979 were stamped with a \"D\" for the Denver mint, an \"S\" for the San Francisco mint (proof coins only), or blank for Philadelphia."]], "year": 1796},
