	"silv_1918_7_s_standing_liberty_quar": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "25 cents (.25 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "6.25 g"], ["DIAMETER", "24.3 mm"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["METAL", ".900 silver, .100 copper"], ["SILVER", ".18084 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Standing Liberty quarter is a 25-cent coin that was struck by the United States Mint from 1916 to 1930. It succeeded the Barber quarter, which had been minted since 1892."], ["THE STORY", "By the time dies were finally made, the year 1916 was almost over, and only 52,000 quarters were struck. So far as I know. there is no thought of issuing new coins of the 50-cent, 25-cent, and 10-cent values."], ["MINTING", "The modification meant that the 1927-S, with a mintage of 396,000 is much cheaper in circulated grades than the 1923-S, with a mintage of 1,360,000, though the 1927-S is more expensive in uncirculated grades."], ["THE DESIGN", "Doscher became well known as \"the girl on the quarter\"; she died in 1970 at age 88."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The United States Mint in 2015 announced plans to restrike for collectors, in gold, the three silver coins first issued in 1916. He wrote to von Engelken on January 6, enclosing a $5 money order, and was sent 20 of the new pieces."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "On January 17, the Mint released the first Standing Liberty quarters, dated both 1916 and 1917, into circulation."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Throughout late 1916, the Mint was intensely busy first sharpening the design to be used in 1917, and then in large-scale preparation of dies to begin striking the new quarters on a massive scale once the new year began."]], "year": 1916},
