	"silv_1901_s_barber_quarter": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "10 cents (0.10 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "2.500 g"], ["DIAMETER", "17.91 mm (0.705 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "1.8 mm"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["SILVER", "0.07234 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "By the late 1880s, there were increasing calls for the replacement of the Seated Liberty design, used since the 1830s on most denominations of silver coins."], ["THE STORY", "The first Barbur coins were struck at the Philadelphia Mint on January 2, 1892, at 9:00 a.m. On December 11, Bosbyshell requested a delay in production to mid-January 1892 to allow the dies to be more thoroughly tested; Leech refused."], ["MINTING", "Finally, large quantities of lower grade Barbur coins were melted for bullion when silver prices rose in 1979 and early 1980. Key dates for the dime include the 1895-O (with the lowest mintage), 1896-S, 1897-O, 1901-S and 1903-S."], ["THE DESIGN", "Each coin could thereafter be altered from the 25th year after it was first produced; for example, a coin first struck in 1892 would be eligible for redesign in 1916."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "One sold for $1,552,500 at auction in 2007."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Although Barbur had warned the director that reputable artists would likely not enter a contest in which only the winner received compensation, Leech offered a $500 prize to the winner, and no payments to anyone else."], ["ONE MORE THING", "As only the winner would receive a cash prize, invited artists refused to participate and no entry from the public proved suitable."]], "year": 1891},
	"silv_1913_s_barber_quarter": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "10 cents (0.10 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "2.500 g"], ["DIAMETER", "17.91 mm (0.705 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "1.8 mm"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["SILVER", "0.07234 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "As only the winner would receive a cash prize, invited artists refused to participate and no entry from the public proved suitable."], ["THE STORY", "The first Barbur coins were struck at the Philadelphia Mint on January 2, 1892, at 9:00 a.m. On December 11, Bosbyshell requested a delay in production to mid-January 1892 to allow the dies to be more thoroughly tested; Leech refused."], ["MINTING", "Finally, large quantities of lower grade Barbur coins were melted for bullion when silver prices rose in 1979 and early 1980. Key dates for the dime include the 1895-O (with the lowest mintage), 1896-S, 1897-O, 1901-S and 1903-S."], ["THE DESIGN", "Each coin could thereafter be altered from the 25th year after it was first produced; for example, a coin first struck in 1892 would be eligible for redesign in 1916."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The rarest half dollar is the 1892-O \"Micro O\", in which the mint mark \"O\" for New Orleans was impressed on the half dollar die with a puncheon intended for the quarter; other key dates are the regular 1892-O, 1892-S, 1893-S, 1897-O, 1897-S, 1913, 1914, and 1915."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Mint had claimed authority under the Coinage Act of 1873 in issuing the Morgan dollar in 1878 and the Liberty Head nickel in 1883."], ["ONE MORE THING", "\"Condition rarities\", relatively common and inexpensive in circulated condition but costly in high grades, include the 1901-S, 1904-S, and 1907-S half dollars."]], "year": 1891},
	"silv_1892_o_micro_o_barber_half_dol": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "10 cents (0.10 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "2.500 g"], ["DIAMETER", "17.91 mm (0.705 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "1.8 mm"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["SILVER", "0.07234 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Leech instructed Barbur to prepare new designs for the dime, quarter, and half dollar, and after the chief engraver made changes to secure Leech's endorsement, they were approved by President Benjamin Harrison in November 1891."], ["THE STORY", "Mason sent Leech the recommendations on April 3, 1891; the following day, the Mint director announced the competition, open to the public, but he specifically invited the ten artists named by Mason to participate."], ["MINTING", "Each coin could thereafter be altered from the 25th year after it was first produced; for example, a coin first struck in 1892 would be eligible for redesign in 1916."], ["THE DESIGN", "Public dissatisfaction with the newly-issued Morgan dollar led the Mint's engravers to submit designs for the smaller silver coins in 1879. For much of the second half of the 19th century, most U.S. silver coins bore a design of a seated Liberty."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "According to Nancy Oliver and Richard Kelly in their 2011 article for The Numismatist, the San Francisco Mint in June 1894 needed to coin $2.40 in silver left over from the melting of worn-out coins, just enough to coin 24 dimes."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first Barbur coins were struck at the Philadelphia Mint on January 2, 1892, at 9:00 a.m."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Key dates for the dime include the 1895-O (with the lowest mintage), 1896-S, 1897-O, 1901-S and 1903-S."]], "year": 1891},
	"silv_1938_d_walking_liberty_half_do": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "50 cents (0.50 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "12.50 g"], ["DIAMETER", "30.63 mm (1.2059 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "1.8 mm"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["SILVER", "0.36169 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Walking Liberty half dollar is a silver 50-cent piece or half dollar coin that was issued by the United States Mint from 1916 to 1947; it was designed by Adolph A. Weinmano, a well-known sculptor and engraver."], ["THE STORY", "So far as I know. there is no thought of issuing new coins of the 50-cent, 25-cent, and 10-cent values."], ["MINTING", "The 1916 pieces were struck in very small numbers—Breen stated that he had seen only four—and only three 1917 proof coins are confirmed, most likely struck for VIPs at a time when proof coins were not sold to the public."], ["THE DESIGN", "Mercanti noted that Weinmano's original plaster was only 6 inches (150 mm) in diameter, and was softly modeled."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Polasek, who received his participation fee of $300 (equal to $ 8,400 in 2023 adjusted for inflation) on March 10, never again had any connection with the Mint or coinage design."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "No Walking Liberty half dollar is especially rare, but many dates are scarce in mint state condition, particularly the 1921 and 1921-D."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The United States Mint in 2015 announced plans to restrike, in gold for collectors, the three silver coins first issued in 1916."]], "year": 1916},
	"silv_1934_s_peace_dollar": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1.00 United States dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "26.73 g (412.5 gr)"], ["DIAMETER", "38.1 mm (1.5 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "2.4 (1921–1935) mm"], ["EDGE", "Reeded"], ["METAL", "copper, silver"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Peace dollar is a United States dollar coin minted for circulation from 1921 to 1928 and 1934 to 1935, and beginning again for collectors in 2021. Designed by Anthony de Francisci, the coin was the result of a competition to find designs emblematic of peace."], ["THE STORY", "Only 270,232,722 coins were melted for sale to the British, but this represented 47% of all Morgan dollars struck to that point. When Congress returned, Vestal asked for unanimous consent that the bill pass on August 1, 1921."], ["MINTING", "Two exceptions are the first year of issue 1921 Peace dollar, minted only at the Philadelphia mint and issued in high relief, and the low-mintage 1928-P Peace dollar. The three mints together struck over 84 million pieces in 1922."], ["THE DESIGN", "At age 34, de Francisci was the youngest of the competitors; he was also among the least experienced in the realm of coin design."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Bland–Allison Act, passed by Congress on February 28, 1878, required the Treasury to purchase a minimum of $2 million in domestically mined silver per month and coin it into silver dollars."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The winner of the competition was to receive $1,500 prize money, while all other participants would be given $100."], ["ONE MORE THING", "When the results proved satisfactory, San Francisco began striking its first Peace dollars using the low-relief design on February 13, with Denver initiating production on February 21, and Philadelphia on February 23."]], "year": 1921},
	"silv_1964_accented_hair_kennedy_hal": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "50 cents (.50 US dollar)"], ["WEIGHT", "1971–current: 11.34 g. 1965–1970: 11.50 g. 1964: 12.50 g"], ["DIAMETER", "30.6 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "2.15 mm"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["METAL", "Outer layer of 75% copper, 25% nickel clad to pure.."]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Intended as a memorial to the assassinated 35th president of the United States John F."], ["THE STORY", "The first Kennedy half dollars intended for circulation were struck at the Denver Mint on January 30, 1964, followed by the Philadelphia Mint the next week. A ceremonial first strike was held at both Philadelphia and Denver on February 11, 1964."], ["MINTING", "The obverse may be redesigned in 2027, and after 2030, is still to bear Kennedy's visage."], ["THE DESIGN", "From 2027 to 2030, there will be one reverse design each year depicting Paralympic sports."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 1973, Congress authorized silver-clad collector versions of the Bicentennial coins; in April 1975, the Mint began to strike them."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first silver-clad half dollars were struck at the Denver Mint on December 30, 1965, bearing the date 1965; the date would not be changed for all US coinage until the coin shortage was eased."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Examples of this variety typically sell for significant amounts of money; a 1964 Special Mint Set half dollar sold for $47,000 in 2016."]], "year": 1964},
	"silv_1878_8_tail_feathers_morgan_do": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1 United States dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "26.73 g (412 1 ⁄ 2 gr)"], ["DIAMETER", "38.1 mm (1.5 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "2.4 mm (0.09 in)"], ["EDGE", "Reeded"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1878–1904, 1921, 2021–present"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "It was the first standard silver dollar minted since the passage of the Coinage Act of 1873, which ended the free coining of silver and the production of the previous design, the Seated Liberty dollar."], ["THE STORY", "In 1876, Director of the Mint Henry Linderman began efforts to redesign the nation's silver coins. In total, Moorgan had five sittings with Williams; he declared her profile to be the most perfect he had seen."], ["MINTING", "In order to conform to the Coinage Act of 1837, the Moorgan dollar contained ninety percent silver and ten percent copper, measured 38.1 millimetres (1.50 in) in diameter and weighed 412.5 grains (26.73 g)."], ["THE DESIGN", "On June 15, 2006, legislation was approved that provided for the minting of a silver dollar and a five dollar gold coin in \"commemoration of the Old Mint at San Francisco,\" with surcharges to be given to the San Francisco Museum and Historical Society in an effort to rehabilitate the Old Mint."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "What came to be known as the Bland–Allison Act required that the Treasury purchase between two and four million dollars' worth of silver per month, to be coined into silver dollars at the former silver/gold value ratio of 16:1, meaning that one ounce of gold would be valued the same as sixteen ounces of silver."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first acceptable strike, after adjustments to the press, was coined at 3:17 p.m. at the Philadelphia Mint."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The Denver Mint, established in 1906, struck the coins for only one year, in 1921."]], "year": 1878},
