	"silv_1963_franklin_half_dollar": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "50 cents (0.50 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "12.50 g"], ["DIAMETER", "30.61 mm (1.21 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "1.8 mm"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["SILVER", "0.36169 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Franklin half dollar is a coin that was struck by the United States Mint from 1948 to 1963. The fifty-cent piece pictures Founding Father Benjamin Franklin on the obverse and the Liberty Bell on the reverse."], ["THE STORY", "An 1890 statute forbade the replacement of a coin design without congressional action, unless it had been in service for 25 years, counting the year of first issuance."], ["MINTING", "The new half dollars first went on sale at noon on April 30, 1948, the anniversary of George Washington's 1789 inauguration as President."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The small eagle on the reverse was added as an afterthought, when Mint officials realized that the Coinage Act of 1873 required one to be displayed on all coins of greater value than the dime."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "For example, although more than nine million 1962 halves were struck for circulation, and an additional three million in proof, the coin was more valuable as bullion than in any condition when silver prices reached record levels in 1979–1980."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Approximately 5-10% of the 1958 Philadelphia coinage is Type II, struck from dies which were first used to strike the 1958 proofs."]], "year": 1948},
	"silv_1964_kennedy_half_dollar": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "50 cents (.50 US dollar)"], ["WEIGHT", "1971–current: 11.34 g. 1965–1970: 11.50 g. 1964: 12.50 g"], ["DIAMETER", "30.6 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "2.15 mm"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["METAL", "Outer layer of 75% copper, 25% nickel clad to pure.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Kennedy half dollar, first minted in 1964, is a fifty-cent coin issued by the United States Mint. Intended as a memorial to the assassinated 35th president of the United States John F."], ["THE STORY", "Work was already underway on coinage dies; the use of the already-available designs allowed for the completion of the first dies on January 2, 1964. A ceremonial first strike was held at both Philadelphia and Denver on February 11, 1964."], ["MINTING", "From 2027 to 2030, there will be one reverse design each year depicting Paralympic sports."], ["THE DESIGN", "The obverse may be redesigned in 2027, and after 2030, is still to bear Kennedy's visage."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Examples of this variety typically sell for significant amounts of money; a 1964 Special Mint Set half dollar sold for $47,000 in 2016."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Treasury had initially planned to issue 91 million half dollars for 1964, but raised the number to 141 million."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The first silver-clad half dollars were struck at the Denver Mint on December 30, 1965, bearing the date 1965; the date would not be changed for all US coinage until the coin shortage was eased."]], "year": 1964},
	"silv_1928_standing_liberty_quarter": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "25 cents (.25 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "6.25 g"], ["DIAMETER", "24.3 mm"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["METAL", ".900 silver, .100 copper"], ["SILVER", ".18084 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Standing Liberty quarter is a 25-cent coin that was struck by the United States Mint from 1916 to 1930. It succeeded the Barber quarter, which had been minted since 1892."], ["THE STORY", "By the time dies were finally made, the year 1916 was almost over, and only 52,000 quarters were struck. So far as I know. there is no thought of issuing new coins of the 50-cent, 25-cent, and 10-cent values."], ["MINTING", "The modification meant that the 1927-S, with a mintage of 396,000 is much cheaper in circulated grades than the 1923-S, with a mintage of 1,360,000, though the 1927-S is more expensive in uncirculated grades."], ["THE DESIGN", "Doscher became well known as \"the girl on the quarter\"; she died in 1970 at age 88."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The United States Mint in 2015 announced plans to restrike for collectors, in gold, the three silver coins first issued in 1916. He wrote to von Engelken on January 6, enclosing a $5 money order, and was sent 20 of the new pieces."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "On January 17, the Mint released the first Standing Liberty quarters, dated both 1916 and 1917, into circulation."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Throughout late 1916, the Mint was intensely busy first sharpening the design to be used in 1917, and then in large-scale preparation of dies to begin striking the new quarters on a massive scale once the new year began."]], "year": 1916},
	"silv_1964_washington_quarter": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "25 cents (0.25 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "5.67 g"], ["DIAMETER", "24.3 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "1.75 mm"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["METAL", "Current—cupronickel clad to copper. Prior to 1965—6.25.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Washington quarter is the present quarter dollar or 25-cent piece issued by the United States Mint. The coin was first struck in 1932; the original version was designed by sculptor John Flanagano."], ["THE STORY", "The 200th anniversary of the birth of Washington, the first president of the United States, would occur in 1932, and Congress wished to plan for the event well in advance."], ["MINTING", "About 6.2 million quarters were struck in 1932, of which 5.4 million were coined at the Philadelphia mint. In the first three years of striking (1932, 1934 and 1935), three different varieties of the obverse are known."], ["THE DESIGN", "Although only one American, Abraham Lincoln, had appeared on a circulating US coin by the 1920s, the Houdon bust had been used as the basis of the portrait of Washington on the commemorative Lafayette dollar dated 1900 and on the Sesquicentennial half dollar of 1926."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The San Francisco Mint had reopened, but from 1968, it struck quarters only for collectors, for the most part proof coins."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "All five 2012 designs were struck, the first circulation-quality coins struck at San Francisco since 1983 (when Lincoln cents were struck without mint mark), and the first with the S mint mark since the Anthony dollar in 1981 (struck for mint sets only)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The Mint estimated that the government profited by $3 billion through seignorage on coins saved by the public and through other revenues, over what it would otherwise have earned."]], "year": 1932},
	"silv_1943_mercury_dime": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "10 cents (0.10 U.S. dollar)"], ["WEIGHT", "2.500 g"], ["DIAMETER", "17.91 mm (0.705 in)"], ["EDGE", "118 reeds"], ["METAL", "0.900 silver, 0.100 copper"], ["GOLD", "2016 gold commemorative version: 0.9999 fine, diameter.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Mercury dime is a ten-cent coin struck by the United States Mint from late 1916 to 1945."], ["THE STORY", "The Mint had offered only a small prize to the winner, and all invited artists refused to submit entries. So far as I know. there is no thought of issuing new coins of the 50-cent, 25-cent, and 10-cent values."], ["MINTING", "A lawyer and insurance executive, Wallace Stevens later became famous as a poet; Wallace and Elsie Stevens rented an apartment from Weinmano from 1909 to 1916. No dimes were struck for 1922, the first time since 1826 that this had occurred."], ["THE DESIGN", "The low-mintage dates are not rare today as many were hoarded, and 1930- and 1931-dated dimes proved readily available from the banks once the economy improved. On the first day of circulation, quantities sold were limited at banks."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Although no 1923 or 1930 dimes were struck at Denver, specimens appearing to be 1923-D or 1930-D dimes may be encountered."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Sinnock, Morgan's successor, executed the design featuring Roosevelt, which replaced the Mercury dime in 1946, making 1945 the last year in which it was produced."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Striking of dimes at Denver did not resume until well into 1917, making the 1917-D relatively rare as well."]], "year": 1916},
	"silv_1910_barber_dime": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "10 cents (0.10 US dollars)"], ["WEIGHT", "2.500 g"], ["DIAMETER", "17.91 mm (0.705 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "1.8 mm"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["SILVER", "0.07234 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "By the late 1880s, there were increasing calls for the replacement of the Seated Liberty design, used since the 1830s on most denominations of silver coins."], ["THE STORY", "Mason sent Leech the recommendations on April 3, 1891; the following day, the Mint director announced the competition, open to the public, but he specifically invited the ten artists named by Mason to participate."], ["MINTING", "Key dates for the dime include the 1895-O (with the lowest mintage), 1896-S, 1897-O, 1901-S and 1903-S. Finally, large quantities of lower grade Barbur coins were melted for bullion when silver prices rose in 1979 and early 1980."], ["THE DESIGN", "Each coin could thereafter be altered from the 25th year after it was first produced; for example, a coin first struck in 1892 would be eligible for redesign in 1916."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "According to Nancy Oliver and Richard Kelly in their 2011 article for The Numismatist, the San Francisco Mint in June 1894 needed to coin $2.40 in silver left over from the melting of worn-out coins, just enough to coin 24 dimes."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Although Barbur had warned the director that reputable artists would likely not enter a contest in which only the winner received compensation, Leech offered a $500 prize to the winner, and no payments to anyone else."], ["ONE MORE THING", "One sold for $1,552,500 at auction in 2007."]], "year": 1891},
	"silv_1986_american_silver_eagle": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1.00 U.S. dollar (face value)"], ["WEIGHT", "31.103 g (1.00 troy oz)"], ["DIAMETER", "40.6 mm (1.598 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "2.98 mm (0.1173 in)"], ["EDGE", "Reeded"], ["METAL", "99.9% Ag"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The American Silver Eagle is the official silver bullion coin of the United States. It was first released by the United States Mint on November 24, 1986, and portrays the Goddess of Liberty in a design by Adolph A."], ["THE STORY", "In July 1981, the House and Senate agreed to allow the sale of 75% of the stockpiled silver (105.1 million troy ounces) over a three-year period, and in September the price of silver fell 11% in response."], ["MINTING", "On March 28, 2020, the West Point Mint, believed to be the only producer of bullion American Silver Eagles from 2018 up until early 2020, was shut down for cleaning after an employee tested positive for SARS-CoV-2."], ["THE DESIGN", "Weinmano, which originally had been used on the Walking Liberty Half Dollar coin of the United States from 1916 to 1947."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Most dates of the bullion issue are not particularly expensive (around $25 as of September 2016) and are traded at a premium above the intrinsic value of the silver they contain; most proof versions (around $65–$75 as of 2016) and uncirculated versions (around $25–$75 as of 2016) sell for more."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "This low mintage beat the record set by the 1995-W Proof American Eagle that was released only with the 10th Anniversary Gold Eagle Proof Set and is the lowest mintage of all Type 1 Silver Eagles."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Some issues sell for significant sums, for example, the 1995-W proof ($3,800 as of 2016) and the 2006 20th anniversary set containing a special \"Reverse Proof\" coin along with a regular proof coin and the new \"Burnished Uncirculated\" coin ($250 as of 2016)."]], "year": 1986},
	"silv_1971_s_eisenhower_dollar": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1 U.S. dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "Copper/nickel-clad: ~22.68 g (350 gr) Silver clad:.."], ["DIAMETER", "38.1 mm (1.5 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "2.58 mm (0.1 in)"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["METAL", "Circulation strikes: outer layers of 75.0% copper 25.0%.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Eisenhower dollar is a one-dollar coin issued by the United States Mint from 1971 to 1978; it was the first coin of that denomination issued by the Mint since the Peace dollar series ended in 1935. The coin depicts President Dwight D."], ["THE STORY", "The Peace dollar, the last circulating dollar made of silver, was not struck after 1935, and in most years in the quarter century after that, the bullion value of a silver dollar did not exceed 70 cents."], ["MINTING", "On June 19, 1945, Gazparro had been one of more than 4 million people who gathered in New York to watch a parade celebrating the Allied victory in Europe."], ["THE DESIGN", "Although Gazparro, then an assistant engraver at the Mint, only saw a glimpse of General Eisenhower, he stepped back from the crowd and drew the general's features."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Coin collectors felt that this set a bad precedent, but about $9 million was paid to the college between 1974 and 1978; yet, despite the infusion of money, the college closed its doors in 1982."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "More than 11 million of the 1971-S silver pieces were sold, in proof and uncirculated, with over 4 million in proof."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Two prototype dollars were struck at the Philadelphia Mint on January 25, 1971; they were subsequently destroyed."]], "year": 1971},
	"gold_1854_s_liberty_head_quarter_ea": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "20 United States dollars"], ["WEIGHT", "33.431 g"], ["DIAMETER", "34.1 mm (1.342 in)"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper"], ["GOLD", ".96750 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Liberty Head double eagle or Coronet double eagle is an American twenty-dollar gold piece struck as a pattern coin in 1849, and for commerce from 1850 to 1907."], ["THE STORY", "Breen, however, stated that the first production coins appeared about January 26, 1850. Under the Mint Act of 1792, the largest-denomination coin was the gold eagle, or ten-dollar piece."], ["MINTING", "The gold dollar and twenty-dollar piece were the first American federal coins on which the designer's initials appear—on the gold dollar, only the \"L\" is used."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "A Philadelphia specimen sold at auction for $1,610,000 in August 2006, setting a record for the Liberty Head double eagle series. Yeoman's A Guide Book of United States Coins lists the 1886 in Very Fine condition (VF-20) at $22,500."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The 1870-CC double eagle, like other coins from that mint's first year of operations, is a great rarity —only 3,789 were struck."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Although many of the double eagles exported in bulk in the late 19th and early 20th centuries were melted—records show that most sent to the United Kingdom were recoined into sovereigns —millions remained in banks."]], "year": 1849},
	"gold_1870_s_indian_princess_three_d": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "3 United States dollars"], ["WEIGHT", "5.015 g"], ["DIAMETER", "20.5 mm (.807 in)"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper"], ["GOLD", ".1451 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The obverse bears a representation of Lady Liberty wearing a headdress of a Native American princess and the reverse a wreath of corn, wheat, cotton, and tobacco."], ["THE STORY", "Much of what is known of the design process for the three-dollar piece is from an August 21, 1858, letter from the Mint's chief engraver, James B."], ["MINTING", "The first three-dollar pieces were 15 proof coins, delivered to the Secretary of the Treasury, James Guthrie, by Mint Director Snowden on April 28, 1854, most likely for distribution to legislators."], ["THE DESIGN", "The coin weighed 64.5 grams, and had a fineness of 900."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The only unique regular-issue U.S. gold piece by date and mint mark, it last came on the market in 1982, when it sold for $687,500."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "An 1855-S in proof is the record holder in sales price for the denomination, selling at auction in 2011 for $1,322,500."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Yeoman's A Guide Book of United States Coins lists the 1854 as the cheapest three-dollar piece in the lowest listed condition (Very Fine or MS-20) at $825."]], "year": 1854},
	"gold_1879_flowing_hair_stella": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Flowing Hair coinage was issued in the United States between 1793 and 1795. The design was used for the first half dime, half dollar, dollar, and the first two large cents."]], "year": 0},
	"gold_1880_coiled_hair_stella": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "4 United States dollars"], ["WEIGHT", "7 g"], ["DIAMETER", "22 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "1.52 mm"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["METAL", "85.7% gold, 4.3% silver, 10% copper"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Two varieties of the Stella were made: Liberty with flowing hair, designed by Charles E."], ["THE STORY", "Five examples of a pattern quintuple stella denominated at 20 dollars were produced in 1879 as well. All 1880 coins are rare; 25 examples are known."]], "year": 1879},
	"gold_1797_draped_bust_eagle": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "\" Draped Bust \" was the name given to a design of United States coins. It appeared on much of the regular-issue copper and silver United States coinage, 1796–1807."], ["MINTING", "In 1796, Congress responded to the almost universal dissatisfaction of the first coins (Flowing Hair dollar) and decreed a new design."], ["THE DESIGN", "The famous 1804 silver dollar has this design as well the reverse of the Kennedy half dollar in 1964."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The first, for copper coins, features the value of the coin (half cent or one cent) surrounded by a wreath or vine."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "By comparison, the Tennessee State Quarter, one of five struck in 2002, had a mintage of 650,000,000 (the mintage of all state quarters in 2002 was over 3 billion)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Only 3,000 were minted and the vast majority of these were either lost, melted or wore out."]], "year": 0},
	"gold_1795_capped_bust_half_eagle": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The Capped Bust coinage of the United States consisted of a half dime, dime, quarter and half dollar."], ["THE STORY", "The Turban design was used on the gold Quarter Eagle, Half Eagle, and Eagle from 1795 to 1834. The obverse of the 2008 Andrew Jackson's Liberty First Spouse coin and medal features the Capped Bust design."], ["MINTING", "There was also a gold design created by engraver Robert Scot created in 1795, also called the Capped Bust, although it is more popularly known as the \" Turban Head \" because of its unusual, exotic appearance."]], "year": 0},
	"gold_1854_o_liberty_double_eagle": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "20 United States dollars"], ["WEIGHT", "33.431 g"], ["DIAMETER", "34.1 mm (1.342 in)"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper"], ["GOLD", ".96750 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Liberty Head double eagle or Coronet double eagle is an American twenty-dollar gold piece struck as a pattern coin in 1849, and for commerce from 1850 to 1907."], ["THE STORY", "Breen, however, stated that the first production coins appeared about January 26, 1850. Under the Mint Act of 1792, the largest-denomination coin was the gold eagle, or ten-dollar piece."], ["MINTING", "The gold dollar and twenty-dollar piece were the first American federal coins on which the designer's initials appear—on the gold dollar, only the \"L\" is used."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "A Philadelphia specimen sold at auction for $1,610,000 in August 2006, setting a record for the Liberty Head double eagle series. Yeoman's A Guide Book of United States Coins lists the 1886 in Very Fine condition (VF-20) at $22,500."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The 1870-CC double eagle, like other coins from that mint's first year of operations, is a great rarity —only 3,789 were struck."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Although many of the double eagles exported in bulk in the late 19th and early 20th centuries were melted—records show that most sent to the United Kingdom were recoined into sovereigns —millions remained in banks."]], "year": 1849},
	"gold_1861_paquet_reverse_double_eag": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "$20"], ["DIAMETER", "34 mm"], ["METAL", "copper, gold"], ["DESIGNER", "Augustus Saint-Gaudenz, James B. Longakre"], ["BIG SALE", "$7,590,020"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Before, the most valuable American coin was the $10 gold eagle, first produced in 1795, two years after the United States Mint opened."], ["MINTING", "On July 20, 2011, a civil-court jury awarded ownership of the ten coins to the U.S. government on grounds that the coins were stolen from the mint. The period in the \"3.5\" was omitted in error on the first copper coin struck."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The 1933 double eagle is among the most valuable of U.S. coins, with the sole example currently known to be in private hands–the King Farouk specimen, which was purchased by King Farouk of Egypt in 1944–selling in 2002 for $7,590,020 and resold to an unknown buyer in 2021 for $18.8 million."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "With the rising price of gold by June it had climbed to $1339, and by December to $1489."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The early 1907 double eagles and the 1986–1991 gold American eagles are the only instances of Roman numerals denoting the date on American coinage."]], "year": 0},
	"gold_1927_d_saint_gaudens_double_ea": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "20 United States dollars"], ["WEIGHT", "33.431 g"], ["DIAMETER", "34.1 mm (1.342 in)"], ["EDGE", "lettered \"E PLURIBUS UNUM\""], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper; 99.99% gold (2009)"], ["GOLD", ".96750 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Saint-Gaudenz double eagle is a twenty- dollar gold coin, or double eagle, produced by the United States Mint from 1907 to 1933. The coin is named after its designer, the sculptor Augustus Saint-Gaudenz, who designed the obverse and reverse."], ["THE STORY", "The Mint had offered only a small prize to the winner, and all invited artists (including Saint-Gaudenz himself) refused to submit entries."], ["MINTING", "Despite a mintage of almost 1.8 million pieces of the 1929 double eagle, it is estimated that fewer than 2,000 exist today, with all the rest melted by the government in the late 1930s."], ["THE DESIGN", "Saint-Gaudenz had produced the models for these dies under the misapprehension that the first pieces had been struck on a production press, rather than on the Mint's only medal press, and therefore only slight adjustment need be made."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Fewer than a thousand are known to have survived; one, in almost-perfect condition (graded MS -67) sold in 2005 for $287,500. In 2002 this coin sold at auction by Sotheboys for $7,590,020."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "These patterns are today known as the \"Ultra High Relief\" or \"Extra High Relief\" pieces, and only about 20 are known —one sold in a 2005 auction for $2,990,000."], ["ONE MORE THING", "According to numismatic historian Roger Burdette, the first 1933 double eagles were struck on March 2, 1933."]], "year": 1907},
	"gold_1796_no_stars_quarter_eagle": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "$2.5"], ["DIAMETER", "18 mm"], ["METAL", "copper, gold"], ["DESIGNER", "Robert Skot"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The quarter eagle is a gold coin that was issued by the United States with a value of two hundred and fifty cents, or two dollars and fifty cents."], ["THE STORY", "The soon-to-follow Coinage Act of 1837 established a fineness of.900, meaning that 1837 and later quarter eagles contain 0.121 troy ounces (0.133 oz; 3.8 g) of gold content. The first issues weighed 67.5 grains (0.154 oz; 4.37 g) at.9167 fineness."], ["MINTING", "This design was minted for 1808 only, but in 1821 the mint reinstated the quarter eagle and it was produced again until 1827, slightly scaled down to 18.5 millimetres (0.73 in) from the original 20."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Only 1,389 of these coins were minted and are highly sought after by collectors. There are several specimens with proof -like surfaces and the coins are highly sought after by collectors, with one example selling for $402,500 at auction in 2006."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Years were skipped at the various mints, with no coins at all made from 1809–1820 and 1916–1924."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 1829, the quarter eagle was reduced in size again to 18.2 millimetres (0.72 in), and featured smaller letters and stars."]], "year": 0},
	"gold_1907_rolled_edge_indian_eagle": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "$10"], ["WEIGHT", "17.5 g"], ["DIAMETER", "26.8 mm"], ["METAL", "copper, gold"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The eagle base-unit of denomination served as the basis of the quarter eagle ($2.50), half eagle ($5), eagle ($10), and double eagle ($20) coins."], ["MINTING", "Under U.S. law (Coinage Act of 1792), the alloy was composed only of silver and copper, with silver limited to no more than half of the alloy by weight. The eagle is a United States $10 gold coin issued by the United States Mint from 1795 to 1933."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The weight of circulating, standard gold, $10 eagles was set at 270 grains (17 g), half eagles at 135 grains (8.7 g), quarter eagles at 67.5 grains (4.37 g). This resulted in the $10 eagle containing 0.5156 troy ounces (16.04 g) of pure gold."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Between 1838 and 1840, the silver content was reduced to zero—the eagle in 1838, half eagle in 1839, and quarter eagle in 1840, — resulting in U.S. gold coins being 90% gold and 10% copper."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Originally the purity of all circulating gold coins in the United States was eleven twelfths pure gold (the same 22 karats level as English crown gold) and one twelfth alloy."]], "year": 0},
	"gold_1920_s_indian_head_eagle": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "10 United States dollars (1 eagle)"], ["WEIGHT", "16.718 g"], ["DIAMETER", "26.92 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "2.023 mm"], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper"], ["GOLD", ".48375 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Indian Head eagle is a $10 gold piece or eagle that was struck by the United States Mint continuously from 1907 until 1916, and then irregularly until 1933. The obverse and reverse were designed by sculptor Augustus Saint-Gaudenz, originally commissioned for use on other denominations."], ["THE STORY", "The Liberty Head design had been first struck for the eagle in 1838; the last addition to the Liberty Head gold series was the double eagle, first struck for circulation in 1850."], ["MINTING", "Subsequently, Indian Head eagles were struck only in 1920 (at San Francisco), 1926 (at Philadelphia), 1930 (at San Francisco), and final Philadelphia issues in 1932 and 1933."], ["THE DESIGN", "The bust of Harriet Eugenia Anderson also inspired Saint-Gaudenz in his model and bas-relief ΝΙΚΗ ΕΙΡΗΝΗ (Greek for victory and peace)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "At Roosevelt's direction, the Mint hired Saint-Gaudenz to redesign the cent and the four gold pieces: the double eagle ($20), eagle ($10), half eagle ($5), and quarter eagle ($2.50). One sold at auction in January 2011 for $230,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "On November 9, 1907, with the dies made from the low relief Saint-Gaudenz models in full production, Frank Leach, the new Mint director, decided to have 31,950 of the rounded rim specimens melted, saving only fifty."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Not all quantities are known, but the highest for which the number struck is known is 1910, with a mintage of 204 (one sold for $80,500 in 2006)."]], "year": 1907},
	"gold_1933_indian_head_eagle": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "10 United States dollars (1 eagle)"], ["WEIGHT", "16.718 g"], ["DIAMETER", "26.92 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "2.023 mm"], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper"], ["GOLD", ".48375 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Indian Head eagle is a $10 gold piece or eagle that was struck by the United States Mint continuously from 1907 until 1916, and then irregularly until 1933. The obverse and reverse were designed by sculptor Augustus Saint-Gaudenz, originally commissioned for use on other denominations."], ["THE STORY", "The Liberty Head design had been first struck for the eagle in 1838; the last addition to the Liberty Head gold series was the double eagle, first struck for circulation in 1850."], ["MINTING", "Subsequently, Indian Head eagles were struck only in 1920 (at San Francisco), 1926 (at Philadelphia), 1930 (at San Francisco), and final Philadelphia issues in 1932 and 1933."], ["THE DESIGN", "The bust of Harriet Eugenia Anderson also inspired Saint-Gaudenz in his model and bas-relief ΝΙΚΗ ΕΙΡΗΝΗ (Greek for victory and peace)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "At Roosevelt's direction, the Mint hired Saint-Gaudenz to redesign the cent and the four gold pieces: the double eagle ($20), eagle ($10), half eagle ($5), and quarter eagle ($2.50). One sold at auction in January 2011 for $230,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "On November 9, 1907, with the dies made from the low relief Saint-Gaudenz models in full production, Frank Leach, the new Mint director, decided to have 31,950 of the rounded rim specimens melted, saving only fifty."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Not all quantities are known, but the highest for which the number struck is known is 1910, with a mintage of 204 (one sold for $80,500 in 2006)."]], "year": 1907},
	"gold_1849_mormon_five_dollar": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "$1"], ["METAL", "manganese, nickel, copper"], ["FIRST OUT", "1794"], ["SOLD", "4 million"], ["MINTAGE", "300,000,000"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "While true gold dollars are no longer minted, the Sacagawea, Presidential, and American Innovation dollars are sometimes referred to as golden dollars because of their color."], ["MINTING", "O: New Orleans, Louisiana (1838–1861; 1879–1909). C: Charlotte, North Carolina (gold coins only; 1838–1861)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By 1853, the value of a U.S. silver dollar contained in gold terms, $1.04 of silver, equal to $ 38.09 today. Only 15 silver dollars with the date of 1804 are known to exist; in 1999, one of them sold at auction for more than $4 million."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Succeeding base-metal $1 coins minted from 1971 onwards did not circulate widely as well, the most important reason being the continued circulation of the $1 bill."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The production of large numbers of U.S. gold coins (The first $1 and $20 gold coins were minted in 1849) from the new California mines lowered the price of gold, thereby increasing the value of silver."]], "year": 1794},
	"gold_1855_wass_molitor_fifty_dollar": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "$1"], ["METAL", "manganese, nickel, copper"], ["FIRST OUT", "1794"], ["SOLD", "4 million"], ["MINTAGE", "300,000,000"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "While true gold dollars are no longer minted, the Sacagawea, Presidential, and American Innovation dollars are sometimes referred to as golden dollars because of their color."], ["MINTING", "C: Charlotte, North Carolina (gold coins only; 1838–1861). D: Dahlonega, Georgia (gold coins only; 1838–1861)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Since 1804 was the last recorded year of mintage for both the dollar and $10 Eagle, it was decided that the set would contain examples of those coins dated 1804, as well as the other denominations currently being produced."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 2019, the GAO re-estimated the cost of replacing the $1 bill and found for the first time that it would cause the government to lose between $611 million and $2.6 billion because physical money was being used less, resulting in dollar bills remaining in circulation longer compared to the 2011 analysis."], ["ONE MORE THING", "By 1853, the value of a U.S. silver dollar contained in gold terms, $1.04 of silver, equal to $ 38.09 today."]], "year": 1794},
	"gold_1915_s_panama_pacific_fifty_do": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "$1"], ["METAL", "manganese, nickel, copper"], ["FIRST OUT", "1794"], ["SOLD", "4 million"], ["MINTAGE", "300,000,000"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Dollar coins have never been popular in circulation since their inception."], ["MINTING", "P: Philadelphia, Pennsylvania (produced from 1793 to date, mint mark introduced in 1979). C: Charlotte, North Carolina (gold coins only; 1838–1861)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By 1853, the value of a U.S. silver dollar contained in gold terms, $1.04 of silver, equal to $ 38.09 today. Only 15 silver dollars with the date of 1804 are known to exist; in 1999, one of them sold at auction for more than $4 million."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The production of large numbers of U.S. gold coins (The first $1 and $20 gold coins were minted in 1849) from the new California mines lowered the price of gold, thereby increasing the value of silver."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The current silver price (January 29, 2021) is $27.03 per troy ounce so a silver dollar is worth, in melt value of about US$20.90."]], "year": 1794},
	"gold_1838_c_classic_head_half_eagle": {"stats": [["FIRST OUT", "1808"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "It was introduced for copper coinage in 1808 by engraver John Reich and later redesigned by Chief Engraver William Kneass."], ["MINTING", "In 1840, a smaller head was designed to conform with the appearance of the larger gold coins, therefore making the Classic Head design obsolete."], ["THE DESIGN", "A similar design on gold coinage kept the name \"Classic Head\", but only retained the curly hair."]], "year": 1808},
	"gold_1861_d_liberty_gold_dollar": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1 United States dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "1.672 g"], ["DIAMETER", "For Type 1, 12.7 mm. For Types 2 and 3, 14.3 mm (For.."], ["EDGE", "Reeded"], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper"], ["GOLD", ".04837 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The gold dollar or gold one-dollar piece is a gold coin that was struck as a regular issue by the United States Bureau of the Mint from 1849 to 1889. The coin had three types over its lifetime, all designed by Mint Chief Engraver James B."], ["THE STORY", "The bill passed easily, and met only minimal opposition in the Senate, becoming law on March 3, 1849. In 1831, the first gold dollar was minted, at the private mint of Christopher Bechtler in North Carolina."], ["MINTING", "The Type 2 and 3 gold dollars depict Liberty as a Native American princess, with a fanciful feathered headdress not resembling any worn by any Indian tribe."], ["THE DESIGN", "The Type 1 gold dollar depicts a head of Liberty, facing left, with a coronet or tiara on her head bearing her name."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Approximately five of the 1849-C Open Wreath are known; one, believed the finest surviving specimen, sold at auction for $690,000 in 2004, remaining a record for the gold dollar series as of 2013."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Mint records indicate the first gold dollars were produced on May 7, 1849; Longakre's diary notes state instead that the first were struck on May 8."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Accordingly, although 1,361,355 gold dollars were struck in 1862—the last time production would exceed a million—the mintage fell to 6,200 in 1863 and remained low for the rest of the coin's existence, excepting 1873 and 1874."]], "year": 1849},
	"gold_1875_liberty_head_gold_dollar": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1 United States dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "1.672 g"], ["DIAMETER", "For Type 1, 12.7 mm. For Types 2 and 3, 14.3 mm (For.."], ["EDGE", "Reeded"], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper"], ["GOLD", ".04837 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The coin had three types over its lifetime, all designed by Mint Chief Engraver James B."], ["THE STORY", "Patterson had more of Gobrecht's pattern dollar struck to show to committee members, again advising against a coin that if issued would be only about a half inch (13 mm) in diameter."], ["MINTING", "The Type 2 and 3 gold dollars depict Liberty as a Native American princess, with a fanciful feathered headdress not resembling any worn by any Indian tribe."], ["THE DESIGN", "The Type 1 gold dollar depicts a head of Liberty, facing left, with a coronet or tiara on her head bearing her name."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Approximately five of the 1849-C Open Wreath are known; one, believed the finest surviving specimen, sold at auction for $690,000 in 2004, remaining a record for the gold dollar series as of 2013."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Mint records indicate the first gold dollars were produced on May 7, 1849; Longakre's diary notes state instead that the first were struck on May 8."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Accordingly, although 1,361,355 gold dollars were struck in 1862—the last time production would exceed a million—the mintage fell to 6,200 in 1863 and remained low for the rest of the coin's existence, excepting 1873 and 1874."]], "year": 1849},
	"gold_1911_d_indian_head_quarter_eag": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "10 United States dollars (1 eagle)"], ["WEIGHT", "16.718 g"], ["DIAMETER", "26.92 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "2.023 mm"], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper"], ["GOLD", ".48375 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Indian Head eagle is a $10 gold piece or eagle that was struck by the United States Mint continuously from 1907 until 1916, and then irregularly until 1933. The obverse and reverse were designed by sculptor Augustus Saint-Gaudenz, originally commissioned for use on other denominations."], ["THE STORY", "The Liberty Head design had been first struck for the eagle in 1838; the last addition to the Liberty Head gold series was the double eagle, first struck for circulation in 1850."], ["MINTING", "Subsequently, Indian Head eagles were struck only in 1920 (at San Francisco), 1926 (at Philadelphia), 1930 (at San Francisco), and final Philadelphia issues in 1932 and 1933."], ["THE DESIGN", "The bust of Harriet Eugenia Anderson also inspired Saint-Gaudenz in his model and bas-relief ΝΙΚΗ ΕΙΡΗΝΗ (Greek for victory and peace)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "At Roosevelt's direction, the Mint hired Saint-Gaudenz to redesign the cent and the four gold pieces: the double eagle ($20), eagle ($10), half eagle ($5), and quarter eagle ($2.50). One sold at auction in January 2011 for $230,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "On November 9, 1907, with the dies made from the low relief Saint-Gaudenz models in full production, Frank Leach, the new Mint director, decided to have 31,950 of the rounded rim specimens melted, saving only fifty."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Not all quantities are known, but the highest for which the number struck is known is 1910, with a mintage of 204 (one sold for $80,500 in 2006)."]], "year": 1907},
	"gold_1909_o_indian_head_half_eagle": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "10 United States dollars (1 eagle)"], ["WEIGHT", "16.718 g"], ["DIAMETER", "26.92 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "2.023 mm"], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper"], ["GOLD", ".48375 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Indian Head eagle is a $10 gold piece or eagle that was struck by the United States Mint continuously from 1907 until 1916, and then irregularly until 1933. The obverse and reverse were designed by sculptor Augustus Saint-Gaudenz, originally commissioned for use on other denominations."], ["THE STORY", "The Liberty Head design had been first struck for the eagle in 1838; the last addition to the Liberty Head gold series was the double eagle, first struck for circulation in 1850."], ["MINTING", "Subsequently, Indian Head eagles were struck only in 1920 (at San Francisco), 1926 (at Philadelphia), 1930 (at San Francisco), and final Philadelphia issues in 1932 and 1933."], ["THE DESIGN", "The bust of Harriet Eugenia Anderson also inspired Saint-Gaudenz in his model and bas-relief ΝΙΚΗ ΕΙΡΗΝΗ (Greek for victory and peace)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "At Roosevelt's direction, the Mint hired Saint-Gaudenz to redesign the cent and the four gold pieces: the double eagle ($20), eagle ($10), half eagle ($5), and quarter eagle ($2.50). One sold at auction in January 2011 for $230,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "On November 9, 1907, with the dies made from the low relief Saint-Gaudenz models in full production, Frank Leach, the new Mint director, decided to have 31,950 of the rounded rim specimens melted, saving only fifty."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Not all quantities are known, but the highest for which the number struck is known is 1910, with a mintage of 204 (one sold for $80,500 in 2006)."]], "year": 1907},
	"gold_1929_indian_head_half_eagle": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "2.5 United States dollars"], ["WEIGHT", "4.18 g"], ["DIAMETER", "18 mm"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["METAL", ".900 gold, .100 copper"], ["GOLD", ".12094 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "These coins were the last of their denominations to be struck for circulation, ending series that began in the 1790s."], ["THE STORY", "On January 3, 1908, Leach wrote to Hering to inform him that all work would be done by the Mint."], ["MINTING", "The 1925 quarter eagle was struck only at Denver, and it was then struck from 1926 to 1929 only at Philadelphia."], ["THE DESIGN", "The half eagle and quarter eagle are identical in design, and are unique in American coinage in having incuse (engraved, as opposed to bas-relief) designs."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "There are only 15 different Indian Head quarter eagles by date and mintmark; the key is the 1911 struck at Denver (1911-D), which the 2014 A Guide Book of United States Coins values at $2,850 even in well-circulated VF (Very Fine, or 20 on the Mint State scale) condition."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Gold coins not released were melted in the mid-1930s, along with those recalled from banks and private holders, after President Franklin Roosevelt ended the issuance of gold coins in 1933."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Before his death in August 1907, Saint-Gaudens completed designs for the eagle ($10 piece) and double eagle, although both required subsequent work to make them fully suitable for coining."]], "year": 1908},
	"gold_1907_high_relief_double_eagle": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "$20"], ["DIAMETER", "34 mm"], ["METAL", "copper, gold"], ["DESIGNER", "Augustus Saint-Gaudenz, James B. Longakre"], ["BIG SALE", "$7,590,020"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "A double eagle is a gold coin of the United States with a denomination of $20. The eagle, half eagle, and quarter eagle were defined by name in the Act of Congress originally authorizing them."], ["MINTING", "The early 1907 double eagles and the 1986–1991 gold American eagles are the only instances of Roman numerals denoting the date on American coinage. The period in the \"3.5\" was omitted in error on the first copper coin struck."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the summer of 2002, a 1933 double eagle was auctioned off for US$7,590,020 which shattered the old record of $4,140,000 paid at a public auction for an 1804 silver dollar."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The 1933 double eagle is among the most valuable of U.S. coins, with the sole example currently known to be in private hands–the King Farouk specimen, which was purchased by King Farouk of Egypt in 1944–selling in 2002 for $7,590,020 and resold to an unknown buyer in 2021 for $18.8 million."], ["ONE MORE THING", "With the rising price of gold by June it had climbed to $1339, and by December to $1489."]], "year": 0},
	"gold_1921_saint_gaudens_double_eagl": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "20 United States dollars"], ["WEIGHT", "33.431 g"], ["DIAMETER", "34.1 mm (1.342 in)"], ["EDGE", "lettered \"E PLURIBUS UNUM\""], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper; 99.99% gold (2009)"], ["GOLD", ".96750 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Saint-Gaudenz double eagle is a twenty- dollar gold coin, or double eagle, produced by the United States Mint from 1907 to 1933. The coin is named after its designer, the sculptor Augustus Saint-Gaudenz, who designed the obverse and reverse."], ["THE STORY", "The Mint had offered only a small prize to the winner, and all invited artists (including Saint-Gaudenz himself) refused to submit entries."], ["MINTING", "Despite a mintage of almost 1.8 million pieces of the 1929 double eagle, it is estimated that fewer than 2,000 exist today, with all the rest melted by the government in the late 1930s."], ["THE DESIGN", "Saint-Gaudenz had produced the models for these dies under the misapprehension that the first pieces had been struck on a production press, rather than on the Mint's only medal press, and therefore only slight adjustment need be made."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Fewer than a thousand are known to have survived; one, in almost-perfect condition (graded MS -67) sold in 2005 for $287,500. In 2002 this coin sold at auction by Sotheboys for $7,590,020."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "These patterns are today known as the \"Ultra High Relief\" or \"Extra High Relief\" pieces, and only about 20 are known —one sold in a 2005 auction for $2,990,000."], ["ONE MORE THING", "According to numismatic historian Roger Burdette, the first 1933 double eagles were struck on March 2, 1933."]], "year": 1907},
	"gold_1932_saint_gaudens_double_eagl": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "20 United States dollars"], ["WEIGHT", "33.431 g"], ["DIAMETER", "34.1 mm (1.342 in)"], ["EDGE", "lettered \"E PLURIBUS UNUM\""], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper; 99.99% gold (2009)"], ["GOLD", ".96750 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Saint-Gaudenz double eagle is a twenty- dollar gold coin, or double eagle, produced by the United States Mint from 1907 to 1933. The coin is named after its designer, the sculptor Augustus Saint-Gaudenz, who designed the obverse and reverse."], ["THE STORY", "The Mint had offered only a small prize to the winner, and all invited artists (including Saint-Gaudenz himself) refused to submit entries."], ["MINTING", "Despite a mintage of almost 1.8 million pieces of the 1929 double eagle, it is estimated that fewer than 2,000 exist today, with all the rest melted by the government in the late 1930s."], ["THE DESIGN", "Saint-Gaudenz had produced the models for these dies under the misapprehension that the first pieces had been struck on a production press, rather than on the Mint's only medal press, and therefore only slight adjustment need be made."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Fewer than a thousand are known to have survived; one, in almost-perfect condition (graded MS -67) sold in 2005 for $287,500. In 2002 this coin sold at auction by Sotheboys for $7,590,020."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "These patterns are today known as the \"Ultra High Relief\" or \"Extra High Relief\" pieces, and only about 20 are known —one sold in a 2005 auction for $2,990,000."], ["ONE MORE THING", "According to numismatic historian Roger Burdette, the first 1933 double eagles were struck on March 2, 1933."]], "year": 1907},
	"gold_1883_liberty_head_double_eagle": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "20 United States dollars"], ["WEIGHT", "33.431 g"], ["DIAMETER", "34.1 mm (1.342 in)"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper"], ["GOLD", ".96750 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Liberty Head double eagle or Coronet double eagle is an American twenty-dollar gold piece struck as a pattern coin in 1849, and for commerce from 1850 to 1907."], ["THE STORY", "Breen, however, stated that the first production coins appeared about January 26, 1850. Under the Mint Act of 1792, the largest-denomination coin was the gold eagle, or ten-dollar piece."], ["MINTING", "The gold dollar and twenty-dollar piece were the first American federal coins on which the designer's initials appear—on the gold dollar, only the \"L\" is used."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "A Philadelphia specimen sold at auction for $1,610,000 in August 2006, setting a record for the Liberty Head double eagle series. Yeoman's A Guide Book of United States Coins lists the 1886 in Very Fine condition (VF-20) at $22,500."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The 1870-CC double eagle, like other coins from that mint's first year of operations, is a great rarity —only 3,789 were struck."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Although many of the double eagles exported in bulk in the late 19th and early 20th centuries were melted—records show that most sent to the United Kingdom were recoined into sovereigns —millions remained in banks."]], "year": 1849},
	"gold_1856_s_liberty_head_gold_dolla": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1 United States dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "1.672 g"], ["DIAMETER", "For Type 1, 12.7 mm. For Types 2 and 3, 14.3 mm (For.."], ["EDGE", "Reeded"], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper"], ["GOLD", ".04837 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Congress was finally galvanized into action by the increased supply of bullion caused by the California gold rush, and in 1849 authorized a gold dollar."], ["THE STORY", "Even before 1848, record amounts of gold were flowing to American mints to be struck into coin, but the California Gold Rush vastly increased these quantities."], ["MINTING", "The Type 2 and 3 gold dollars depict Liberty as a Native American princess, with a fanciful feathered headdress not resembling any worn by any Indian tribe."], ["THE DESIGN", "The Type 1 gold dollar depicts a head of Liberty, facing left, with a coronet or tiara on her head bearing her name."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Those seeking one of each type will find the most expensive to be a specimen of the Type 2, with the 1854 and 1855 estimated at $350 in that condition; the other two types have dates valued at $300 in that grade."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Accordingly, although 1,361,355 gold dollars were struck in 1862—the last time production would exceed a million—the mintage fell to 6,200 in 1863 and remained low for the rest of the coin's existence, excepting 1873 and 1874."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Of the coins struck at the branch mints in 1849, only pieces struck at Charlotte (1849-C) exist in multiple varieties; most are of what is dubbed the \"Closed Wreath\" variety."]], "year": 1849},
	"gold_1854_indian_princess_three_dol": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "3 United States dollars"], ["WEIGHT", "5.015 g"], ["DIAMETER", "20.5 mm (.807 in)"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper"], ["GOLD", ".1451 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "By some accounts, the coin was created so larger quantities of stamps could be purchased."], ["THE STORY", "White sought a means of returning American gold coins to circulation—as gold was overvalued with respect to silver by the government, gold coins had been routinely exported since the start of the 19th century."], ["MINTING", "However, after June 8, there was only one further delivery by Peale, on November 10, when the last 22,740 of the mintage of 138,618 were delivered."], ["THE DESIGN", "The coin weighed 64.5 grams, and had a fineness of 900."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "These pieces had been made available to the public only as part of a proof set of all gold denominations, at a price of $43 (a premium of $1.50 over face value)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "An 1855-S in proof is the record holder in sales price for the denomination, selling at auction in 2011 for $1,322,500."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The only unique regular-issue U.S. gold piece by date and mint mark, it last came on the market in 1982, when it sold for $687,500."]], "year": 1854},
	"gold_1915_s_panama_pacific_quarter": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "$2.5"], ["DIAMETER", "18 mm"], ["METAL", "copper, gold"], ["DESIGNER", "Robert Skot"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "It was given its name in the Coinage Act of 1792, as a derivation from the US ten-dollar eagle coin."], ["THE STORY", "The soon-to-follow Coinage Act of 1837 established a fineness of.900, meaning that 1837 and later quarter eagles contain 0.121 troy ounces (0.133 oz; 3.8 g) of gold content. The first issues weighed 67.5 grains (0.154 oz; 4.37 g) at.9167 fineness."], ["MINTING", "Relatively few coins were struck prior to 1834, owing to their higher gold content (promoting melting for their bullion content). Only 1,389 of these coins were minted and are highly sought after by collectors."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The quarter eagle denomination was struck at the main mint at Philadelphia (1796–1929), and branch mints in Charlotte (1838–1860), New Orleans (1839–1857 only), Dahlonega (1839–1859), San Francisco (1854–1879), and Denver (1911–1925)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Years were skipped at the various mints, with no coins at all made from 1809–1820 and 1916–1924."]], "year": 0},
	"gold_1903_louisiana_purchase_gold_d": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1 US dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "1.672 g"], ["DIAMETER", "15 mm"], ["EDGE", "Reeded"], ["METAL", "copper, gold"], ["GOLD", "0.04837 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Louisiana Purchase Exposition gold dollar is a commemorative coin issue dated 1903. Struck in two varieties, the coins were designed by United States Bureau of the Mint Chief Engraver Charles E."], ["THE STORY", "Louis sub-treasury on December 22, 1902, to await the organizing committee's compliance with other parts of the law, most likely relating to the required posting of a bond."], ["MINTING", "Despite efforts by Zerbe which Bowers finds \"enthusiastic or even heroic\", only about 35,000 were sold to the public; the remaining 215,000 were returned to the Mint and melted around 1914."], ["THE DESIGN", "Max Mehl deemed the issue \"the most attractive of all of our commemorative gold dollars\"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "After some haggling, they agreed on a price of 60 million francs, plus 20 million more to pay claims by American citizens against France—a total of some $15 million, which paid for some 828,500 square miles (2,146,000 km) of land."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Despite Zerbe's statement that he would support the issue price of the coins at $3, he did not do so and the price of the dollars fell to about $2 by late 1905."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Zerbe stated in 1905 that he \"was the only man to sell 50,000 dollars at $3 apiece\"."]], "year": 1903},
	"gold_1926_sesquicentennial_quarter": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "$2.5"], ["DIAMETER", "18 mm"], ["METAL", "copper, gold"], ["DESIGNER", "Robert Skot"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The quarter eagle is a gold coin that was issued by the United States with a value of two hundred and fifty cents, or two dollars and fifty cents."], ["THE STORY", "The first issues weighed 67.5 grains (0.154 oz; 4.37 g) at.9167 fineness. The quarter eagle denomination was officially discontinued in 1933 with the removal of the United States from the Gold Standard, although the last date of issue was 1929."], ["MINTING", "The quarter eagle denomination was struck at the main mint at Philadelphia (1796–1929), and branch mints in Charlotte (1838–1860), New Orleans (1839–1857 only), Dahlonega (1839–1859), San Francisco (1854–1879), and Denver (1911–1925)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "There are several specimens with proof -like surfaces and the coins are highly sought after by collectors, with one example selling for $402,500 at auction in 2006. Only 1,389 of these coins were minted and are highly sought after by collectors."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Liberty Head design was created by Christian Gobrecht and was produced successfully from 1840 to 1907, the most popular of all of the models."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Years were skipped at the various mints, with no coins at all made from 1809–1820 and 1916–1924."]], "year": 0},
	"gold_1878_liberty_head_eagle": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "$10"], ["WEIGHT", "17.5 g"], ["DIAMETER", "26.8 mm"], ["METAL", "copper, gold"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The eagle base-unit of denomination served as the basis of the quarter eagle ($2.50), half eagle ($5), eagle ($10), and double eagle ($20) coins."], ["MINTING", "The eagle was the largest of the five main decimal base-units of denomination used for circulating coinage in the United States prior to 1933, the year when gold was withdrawn from circulation."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "This resulted in the $10 eagle containing 0.5156 troy ounces (16.04 g) of pure gold. The weight of circulating, standard gold, $10 eagles was set at 270 grains (17 g), half eagles at 135 grains (8.7 g), quarter eagles at 67.5 grains (4.37 g)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 1984, an eagle was issued to commemorate the Summer Olympics, and another eagle was issued in 2003 to commemorate the Wright brothers first flight at Kitty Hawk."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 2000 a unique eagle, the Library of Congress bimetallic eagle, was issued commemorating the Library of Congress; it consisted of equal weights of an approximately 1 ⁄ 4 troy ounce (0.27 oz; 7.8 g).9995 fine platinum core and a.900 fine gold outer ring."]], "year": 0},
	"gold_1899_liberty_head_half_eagle": {"stats": [["FACE VALUE", "5 U.S. dollars (face value)"], ["WEIGHT", "8.75 g"], ["DIAMETER", "22 mm"], ["EDGE", "Reeded?"], ["METAL", "91.67% Au, 3% Ag, 5.33% Cu"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1795–1929 (circulating) 1983–present (bullion coin)"], ["DESIGNER", "William Kneasz"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Production of the half eagle was authorized by the Coinage Act of 1792, and it was the first gold coin minted by the United States."], ["MINTING", "In 1838, the Charlotte Mint and the Dahlonega Mint produced half eagles of the Coronet type in their first years of operation, and continued to mint half eagles until 1861, their last year of operation."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By 1834, the gold in the half eagle had been worth more than its face value for several years. The $5 denomination has the distinction of being the only denomination for which coins were minted at eight US mints."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The weight of the coin was reduced to 8.36 grams, the diameter reduced to 22.5 mm, and the composition changed to.8992 gold and.1008 silver and copper."], ["ONE MORE THING", "On January 18, 1837, the gold content of this type was increased to.900 in accordance with the Coinage Act of 1837."]], "year": 1795},
	"gold_1904_liberty_head_double_eagle": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "20 United States dollars"], ["WEIGHT", "33.431 g"], ["DIAMETER", "34.1 mm (1.342 in)"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper"], ["GOLD", ".96750 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Liberty Head double eagle or Coronet double eagle is an American twenty-dollar gold piece struck as a pattern coin in 1849, and for commerce from 1850 to 1907."], ["THE STORY", "Breen, however, stated that the first production coins appeared about January 26, 1850. Under the Mint Act of 1792, the largest-denomination coin was the gold eagle, or ten-dollar piece."], ["MINTING", "The gold dollar and twenty-dollar piece were the first American federal coins on which the designer's initials appear—on the gold dollar, only the \"L\" is used."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "A Philadelphia specimen sold at auction for $1,610,000 in August 2006, setting a record for the Liberty Head double eagle series. Yeoman's A Guide Book of United States Coins lists the 1886 in Very Fine condition (VF-20) at $22,500."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The 1870-CC double eagle, like other coins from that mint's first year of operations, is a great rarity —only 3,789 were struck."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Although many of the double eagles exported in bulk in the late 19th and early 20th centuries were melted—records show that most sent to the United Kingdom were recoined into sovereigns —millions remained in banks."]], "year": 1849},
	"gold_1924_saint_gaudens_double_eagl": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "20 United States dollars"], ["WEIGHT", "33.431 g"], ["DIAMETER", "34.1 mm (1.342 in)"], ["EDGE", "lettered \"E PLURIBUS UNUM\""], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper; 99.99% gold (2009)"], ["GOLD", ".96750 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Saint-Gaudenz double eagle is a twenty- dollar gold coin, or double eagle, produced by the United States Mint from 1907 to 1933. The coin is named after its designer, the sculptor Augustus Saint-Gaudenz, who designed the obverse and reverse."], ["THE STORY", "The Mint had offered only a small prize to the winner, and all invited artists (including Saint-Gaudenz himself) refused to submit entries."], ["MINTING", "Despite a mintage of almost 1.8 million pieces of the 1929 double eagle, it is estimated that fewer than 2,000 exist today, with all the rest melted by the government in the late 1930s."], ["THE DESIGN", "Saint-Gaudenz had produced the models for these dies under the misapprehension that the first pieces had been struck on a production press, rather than on the Mint's only medal press, and therefore only slight adjustment need be made."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Fewer than a thousand are known to have survived; one, in almost-perfect condition (graded MS -67) sold in 2005 for $287,500. In 2002 this coin sold at auction by Sotheboys for $7,590,020."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "These patterns are today known as the \"Ultra High Relief\" or \"Extra High Relief\" pieces, and only about 20 are known —one sold in a 2005 auction for $2,990,000."], ["ONE MORE THING", "According to numismatic historian Roger Burdette, the first 1933 double eagles were struck on March 2, 1933."]], "year": 1907},
	"gold_1926_indian_head_eagle": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "10 United States dollars (1 eagle)"], ["WEIGHT", "16.718 g"], ["DIAMETER", "26.92 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "2.023 mm"], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper"], ["GOLD", ".48375 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Indian Head eagle is a $10 gold piece or eagle that was struck by the United States Mint continuously from 1907 until 1916, and then irregularly until 1933. The obverse and reverse were designed by sculptor Augustus Saint-Gaudenz, originally commissioned for use on other denominations."], ["THE STORY", "The Liberty Head design had been first struck for the eagle in 1838; the last addition to the Liberty Head gold series was the double eagle, first struck for circulation in 1850."], ["MINTING", "Subsequently, Indian Head eagles were struck only in 1920 (at San Francisco), 1926 (at Philadelphia), 1930 (at San Francisco), and final Philadelphia issues in 1932 and 1933."], ["THE DESIGN", "The bust of Harriet Eugenia Anderson also inspired Saint-Gaudenz in his model and bas-relief ΝΙΚΗ ΕΙΡΗΝΗ (Greek for victory and peace)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "At Roosevelt's direction, the Mint hired Saint-Gaudenz to redesign the cent and the four gold pieces: the double eagle ($20), eagle ($10), half eagle ($5), and quarter eagle ($2.50). One sold at auction in January 2011 for $230,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "On November 9, 1907, with the dies made from the low relief Saint-Gaudenz models in full production, Frank Leach, the new Mint director, decided to have 31,950 of the rounded rim specimens melted, saving only fifty."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Not all quantities are known, but the highest for which the number struck is known is 1910, with a mintage of 204 (one sold for $80,500 in 2006)."]], "year": 1907},
	"gold_1928_indian_head_quarter_eagle": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "2.5 United States dollars"], ["WEIGHT", "4.18 g"], ["DIAMETER", "18 mm"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["METAL", ".900 gold, .100 copper"], ["GOLD", ".12094 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The quarter eagle was struck from 1908 to 1915 and from 1925–1929."], ["THE STORY", "Mint officials originally assumed that whatever design was selected for the double eagle would simply be scaled down for the three lower denominations."], ["MINTING", "Gold coins not released were melted in the mid-1930s, along with those recalled from banks and private holders, after President Franklin Roosevelt ended the issuance of gold coins in 1933."], ["THE DESIGN", "Until Saint-Gaudens' and Pratto's pieces were struck, only Mint Chief Engraver James Longacre had attempted to depict Indians on US circulating coinage (in the 1850s), with his Indian Head cent and Indian Princess designs for the gold dollar and three-dollar pieces."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Originally it was the intention to give the $5 and $2.50 pieces the same design as that used on the double eagle or $20 piece, but before final action to that end was taken President Roosevelt invited me to lunch with him at the White House."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Before his death in August 1907, Saint-Gaudens completed designs for the eagle ($10 piece) and double eagle, although both required subsequent work to make them fully suitable for coining."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Experimental pieces to a total face value of $75 (likely ten half eagles and ten quarter eagles) were sent to Leach in Washington from the Philadelphia Mint on September 21."]], "year": 1908},
	"gold_1901_liberty_head_eagle": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "$10"], ["WEIGHT", "17.5 g"], ["DIAMETER", "26.8 mm"], ["METAL", "copper, gold"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The eagle base-unit of denomination served as the basis of the quarter eagle ($2.50), half eagle ($5), eagle ($10), and double eagle ($20) coins."], ["MINTING", "Gold eagles were issued for circulation by the United States Mint from 1795–1933, half eagles from 1795–1929, quarter eagles from 1796–1929, and double eagles from 1850–1933, with occasional production gaps for each type."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "This resulted in the $10 eagle containing 0.5156 troy ounces (16.04 g) of pure gold. The weight of circulating, standard gold, $10 eagles was set at 270 grains (17 g), half eagles at 135 grains (8.7 g), quarter eagles at 67.5 grains (4.37 g)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 2000 a unique eagle, the Library of Congress bimetallic eagle, was issued commemorating the Library of Congress; it consisted of equal weights of an approximately 1 ⁄ 4 troy ounce (0.27 oz; 7.8 g).9995 fine platinum core and a.900 fine gold outer ring."], ["ONE MORE THING", "As a result, the specification for standard gold was lowered from 22 karat (.9167 fine) to.8992 fine (21.58 kt)."]], "year": 0},
	"gold_1908_indian_head_half_eagle": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "2.5 United States dollars"], ["WEIGHT", "4.18 g"], ["DIAMETER", "18 mm"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["METAL", ".900 gold, .100 copper"], ["GOLD", ".12094 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The quarter eagle was struck from 1908 to 1915 and from 1925–1929."], ["THE STORY", "By the Mint Act of 1792, an \"eagle\" was made equivalent to ten dollars."], ["MINTING", "After Pratto, only James Earle Fraser's depiction of an Indian in 1913 on the Buffalo nickel would appear until the 2000 arrival of the Sacagawea dollar."], ["THE DESIGN", "The half eagle and quarter eagle are identical in design, and are unique in American coinage in having incuse (engraved, as opposed to bas-relief) designs."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "There are only 15 different Indian Head quarter eagles by date and mintmark; the key is the 1911 struck at Denver (1911-D), which the 2014 A Guide Book of United States Coins values at $2,850 even in well-circulated VF (Very Fine, or 20 on the Mint State scale) condition."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Gold coins not released were melted in the mid-1930s, along with those recalled from banks and private holders, after President Franklin Roosevelt ended the issuance of gold coins in 1933."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Before his death in August 1907, Saint-Gaudens completed designs for the eagle ($10 piece) and double eagle, although both required subsequent work to make them fully suitable for coining."]], "year": 1908},
	"gold_1986_american_gold_eagle": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "5–50 U.S. dollars (face value); see specifications"], ["WEIGHT", "33.93 g"], ["DIAMETER", "32.7 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "2.87 mm"], ["EDGE", "Reeded"], ["METAL", "91.67% Au, 3% Ag, 5.33% Cu"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The American Gold Eagle is an official gold bullion coin of the United States. Authorized under the Gold Bullion Coin Act of 1985, it was first released by the United States Mint in 1986."], ["MINTING", "The mintage number is the Last Known Sale (LKS), which is the last sales figure published for that product before it was dropped from the sales report. During the series' inaugural year, the Mint only issued 1 troy oz proofs."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "While their actual selling price (purchasing power) varies based on the spot price of gold, these coins carry face values of $5, $10, $25, and $50. Mint sold the 2016 one-ounce coin ($50 face value) at $1,510.00."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Offered in 1/10 oz, 1/4 oz, 1/2 oz, and 1 oz denominations, these coins are guaranteed by the U.S. government to contain the stated amount of actual gold weight in troy ounces."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Crown gold alloys had not been used in U.S. coins since 1834, with the gold content having dropped since 1837 to a standard of 0.900 fine for U.S. gold coins."]], "year": 1986},
	"gold_1999_american_gold_eagle_tenth": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "$10"], ["WEIGHT", "17.5 g"], ["DIAMETER", "26.8 mm"], ["METAL", "copper, gold"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The eagle base-unit of denomination served as the basis of the quarter eagle ($2.50), half eagle ($5), eagle ($10), and double eagle ($20) coins."], ["MINTING", "The eagle is a United States $10 gold coin issued by the United States Mint from 1795 to 1933. Under U.S. law (Coinage Act of 1792), the alloy was composed only of silver and copper, with silver limited to no more than half of the alloy by weight."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The weight of circulating, standard gold, $10 eagles was set at 270 grains (17 g), half eagles at 135 grains (8.7 g), quarter eagles at 67.5 grains (4.37 g). This resulted in the $10 eagle containing 0.5156 troy ounces (16.04 g) of pure gold."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "As a result, the specification for standard gold was lowered from 22 karat (.9167 fine) to.8992 fine (21.58 kt)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Between 1838 and 1840, the silver content was reduced to zero—the eagle in 1838, half eagle in 1839, and quarter eagle in 1840, — resulting in U.S. gold coins being 90% gold and 10% copper."]], "year": 0},
	"gold_1878_s_liberty_head_eagle": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "$10"], ["WEIGHT", "17.5 g"], ["DIAMETER", "26.8 mm"], ["METAL", "copper, gold"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The eagle base-unit of denomination served as the basis of the quarter eagle ($2.50), half eagle ($5), eagle ($10), and double eagle ($20) coins."], ["MINTING", "The eagle was the largest of the five main decimal base-units of denomination used for circulating coinage in the United States prior to 1933, the year when gold was withdrawn from circulation."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "This resulted in the $10 eagle containing 0.5156 troy ounces (16.04 g) of pure gold. The weight of circulating, standard gold, $10 eagles was set at 270 grains (17 g), half eagles at 135 grains (8.7 g), quarter eagles at 67.5 grains (4.37 g)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Thus, U.S. gold coins had 22/24 (22 kt or 91.667%) pure gold, at most 1/24 (0–4.167%) silver, with the remaining fraction, (4.167–8.333%), copper."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Under U.S. law (Coinage Act of 1792), the alloy was composed only of silver and copper, with silver limited to no more than half of the alloy by weight."]], "year": 0},
	"gold_1910_d_indian_head_quarter_eag": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "10 United States dollars (1 eagle)"], ["WEIGHT", "16.718 g"], ["DIAMETER", "26.92 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "2.023 mm"], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper"], ["GOLD", ".48375 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Indian Head eagle is a $10 gold piece or eagle that was struck by the United States Mint continuously from 1907 until 1916, and then irregularly until 1933. The obverse and reverse were designed by sculptor Augustus Saint-Gaudenz, originally commissioned for use on other denominations."], ["THE STORY", "The Liberty Head design had been first struck for the eagle in 1838; the last addition to the Liberty Head gold series was the double eagle, first struck for circulation in 1850."], ["MINTING", "Subsequently, Indian Head eagles were struck only in 1920 (at San Francisco), 1926 (at Philadelphia), 1930 (at San Francisco), and final Philadelphia issues in 1932 and 1933."], ["THE DESIGN", "The bust of Harriet Eugenia Anderson also inspired Saint-Gaudenz in his model and bas-relief ΝΙΚΗ ΕΙΡΗΝΗ (Greek for victory and peace)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "At Roosevelt's direction, the Mint hired Saint-Gaudenz to redesign the cent and the four gold pieces: the double eagle ($20), eagle ($10), half eagle ($5), and quarter eagle ($2.50). One sold at auction in January 2011 for $230,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "On November 9, 1907, with the dies made from the low relief Saint-Gaudenz models in full production, Frank Leach, the new Mint director, decided to have 31,950 of the rounded rim specimens melted, saving only fifty."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Not all quantities are known, but the highest for which the number struck is known is 1910, with a mintage of 204 (one sold for $80,500 in 2006)."]], "year": 1907},
	"gold_1853_liberty_gold_dollar": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1 United States dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "1.672 g"], ["DIAMETER", "For Type 1, 12.7 mm. For Types 2 and 3, 14.3 mm (For.."], ["EDGE", "Reeded"], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper"], ["GOLD", ".04837 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The gold dollar or gold one-dollar piece is a gold coin that was struck as a regular issue by the United States Bureau of the Mint from 1849 to 1889. The coin had three types over its lifetime, all designed by Mint Chief Engraver James B."], ["THE STORY", "The bill passed easily, and met only minimal opposition in the Senate, becoming law on March 3, 1849. In 1831, the first gold dollar was minted, at the private mint of Christopher Bechtler in North Carolina."], ["MINTING", "The Type 2 and 3 gold dollars depict Liberty as a Native American princess, with a fanciful feathered headdress not resembling any worn by any Indian tribe."], ["THE DESIGN", "The Type 1 gold dollar depicts a head of Liberty, facing left, with a coronet or tiara on her head bearing her name."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Approximately five of the 1849-C Open Wreath are known; one, believed the finest surviving specimen, sold at auction for $690,000 in 2004, remaining a record for the gold dollar series as of 2013."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Mint records indicate the first gold dollars were produced on May 7, 1849; Longakre's diary notes state instead that the first were struck on May 8."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Accordingly, although 1,361,355 gold dollars were struck in 1862—the last time production would exceed a million—the mintage fell to 6,200 in 1863 and remained low for the rest of the coin's existence, excepting 1873 and 1874."]], "year": 1849},
	"gold_1874_indian_princess_three_dol": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "3 United States dollars"], ["WEIGHT", "5.015 g"], ["DIAMETER", "20.5 mm (.807 in)"], ["EDGE", "reeded"], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper"], ["GOLD", ".1451 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Two years later, a bill was passed which authorized a three-dollar coin."], ["THE STORY", "Much of what is known of the design process for the three-dollar piece is from an August 21, 1858, letter from the Mint's chief engraver, James B."], ["MINTING", "Dies were sent to New Orleans in 1855, 1856, 1859, and 1861, only to remain unused; no further strikings took place at any of the three southern branch mints."], ["THE DESIGN", "As Longakre was busy with the reduced-weight silver coins ordered by Congress in the same act that had authorized the three-dollar piece, work on dies did not begin until 1854."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "An 1855-S in proof is the record holder in sales price for the denomination, selling at auction in 2011 for $1,322,500."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "No other three-dollar pieces were struck at San Francisco in 1870; dies were sent there most years between 1861 and 1873, but, with the exception of 1870, were not used."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Although Congress, in passing the Coinage Act of 1834, made adjustments to the ratio between gold and silver, it did not authorize a $3 coin at that time."]], "year": 1854},
	"gold_1907_liberty_head_quarter_eagl": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "$2.5"], ["DIAMETER", "18 mm"], ["METAL", "copper, gold"], ["DESIGNER", "Robert Skot"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The quarter eagle is a gold coin that was issued by the United States with a value of two hundred and fifty cents, or two dollars and fifty cents."], ["THE STORY", "The quarter eagle denomination was officially discontinued in 1933 with the removal of the United States from the Gold Standard, although the last date of issue was 1929. The first issues weighed 67.5 grains (0.154 oz; 4.37 g) at.9167 fineness."], ["MINTING", "The soon-to-follow Coinage Act of 1837 established a fineness of.900, meaning that 1837 and later quarter eagles contain 0.121 troy ounces (0.133 oz; 3.8 g) of gold content."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Only 1,389 of these coins were minted and are highly sought after by collectors. There are several specimens with proof -like surfaces and the coins are highly sought after by collectors, with one example selling for $402,500 at auction in 2006."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "This design was minted for 1808 only, but in 1821 the mint reinstated the quarter eagle and it was produced again until 1827, slightly scaled down to 18.5 millimetres (0.73 in) from the original 20."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 1829, the quarter eagle was reduced in size again to 18.2 millimetres (0.72 in), and featured smaller letters and stars."]], "year": 0},
	"gold_1915_indian_head_quarter_eagle": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "10 United States dollars (1 eagle)"], ["WEIGHT", "16.718 g"], ["DIAMETER", "26.92 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "2.023 mm"], ["METAL", "90% gold, 10% copper"], ["GOLD", ".48375 troy oz"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Indian Head eagle is a $10 gold piece or eagle that was struck by the United States Mint continuously from 1907 until 1916, and then irregularly until 1933. The obverse and reverse were designed by sculptor Augustus Saint-Gaudenz, originally commissioned for use on other denominations."], ["THE STORY", "The Liberty Head design had been first struck for the eagle in 1838; the last addition to the Liberty Head gold series was the double eagle, first struck for circulation in 1850."], ["MINTING", "Subsequently, Indian Head eagles were struck only in 1920 (at San Francisco), 1926 (at Philadelphia), 1930 (at San Francisco), and final Philadelphia issues in 1932 and 1933."], ["THE DESIGN", "The bust of Harriet Eugenia Anderson also inspired Saint-Gaudenz in his model and bas-relief ΝΙΚΗ ΕΙΡΗΝΗ (Greek for victory and peace)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "At Roosevelt's direction, the Mint hired Saint-Gaudenz to redesign the cent and the four gold pieces: the double eagle ($20), eagle ($10), half eagle ($5), and quarter eagle ($2.50). One sold at auction in January 2011 for $230,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "On November 9, 1907, with the dies made from the low relief Saint-Gaudenz models in full production, Frank Leach, the new Mint director, decided to have 31,950 of the rounded rim specimens melted, saving only fifty."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Not all quantities are known, but the highest for which the number struck is known is 1910, with a mintage of 204 (one sold for $80,500 in 2006)."]], "year": 1907},
	"wrld_1937_edward_viii_sovereign": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["SOLD", "1 million"], ["BIG SALE", "$2,280,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Edward VIII coins are a series of coins from the United Kingdom and other territories of the British Empire that were produced in 1936 upon the accession of King Edward VIII."], ["MINTING", "Striking of Edward VIII's coinage was scheduled to begin on 1 January 1937, one month after he abdicated in December 1936. Most of these are dated 1936 even if manufactured years later."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On 26 March 2021, a five pound gold coin sold at auction for $2,280,000, surpassing the previous record. In 2020, an Edward VIII sovereign sold at auction for £1 million, the most for a British coin."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Because of his short reign of just 326 days and eventual abdication, many never entered circulation and remained only as pattern pieces."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Royal Mint reports from 1935–1936 suggest that over 200 dies for coins, medals, and seals had already been produced in preparation."]], "year": 0},
	"wrld_1703_queen_anne_vigo_five_guin": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["FACE VALUE", "£1.05, £1"], ["WEIGHT", "8.4 g"], ["METAL", "gold"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "From 1717 to 1816, its value was officially fixed at twenty-one shillings."], ["THE STORY", "The coin was produced every year between 1663 and 1684, with an elephant appearing on some coins each year from 1663 to 1665 and 1668, and the elephant with a howdah on other coins minted from 1674 or 1675 onwards."], ["MINTING", "In 2013 the Royal Mint issued a £2 coin to celebrate the 350th anniversary of the minting of the first guinea coin."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Bids are still made in guineas for the sale of racehorses at auction, at which the purchaser will pay the guinea-equivalent amount but the seller will receive only that number of pounds."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The guinea was used in a similar way in Australia until that country converted to decimal currency in 1966, after which it became worth $2.10."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The first guinea was produced on 6 February 1663; a proclamation of 27 March 1663 made the coins legal currency."]], "year": 0},
	"wrld_1621_poland_sigismund_iii_100": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The ducat coin was used as a trade coin in Europe from the later Middle Ages to the 19th century."], ["MINTING", "The Austrian Mint still mints single and four-ducats, both dated 1915. The Hungarian mint still mints commemorative coins with 2, 3, 4 and 6-ducats quality."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Continued depreciation in the silver currency during the 16th century, however, made the gold ducat worth more than 124 soldi. At that point a ducat worth 124 soldi emerged as a new silver-based unit of account for quoting salaries and costs."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 1252 Florence and Genoa introduced the gold florin and genovino, respectively, both of 3.5 grams of 98.6% fine gold; the florin preceded the ducat as Western Europe's first standard gold coin."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The Venetian ducat contained 3.545 grams of 99.47% fine gold, the highest purity medieval metallurgy could produce."]], "year": 0},
	"wrld_1907_mexico_50_pesos_pattern": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "Mexico"], ["FIRST OUT", "1863"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The Mexican peso is the 16th most traded currency in the world, the third most traded currency from the Americas (after the United States dollar and Canadian dollar), and the most traded currency from Latin America."], ["THE STORY", "The post-independence silver peso contained 27.07 grams of 90.3% fine silver (24.44 g fine) while the gold peso or half escudo contained 1.6915 grams of 87.5% fine gold (1.48 g fine)."], ["MINTING", "Though the 50- and 100-peso coins are the only currently circulating coinage in the world to contain any silver, they rarely circulate because their silver content of 1/2 troy ounce have exceeded 100 pesos in value since around 2010."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "After the Mexican peso crisis of December 1994, the peso sharply depreciated from MXN$3.4 to MXN$7.2 in 1995. As of 2020, the coins most commonly encountered in circulation have face values of 50¢, $1, $2, $5 and $10."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Series C coins (which dropped the \"N$\" prefix on the MXN$1, N$5, N$10, N$20, and N$50 coins) were circulated starting in 1996 following the withdrawal of Series A and AA coins."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The 500 nuevos pesos note was worth more than US$ 100 when it was introduced, but its value dropped to almost equal to $100 by the end of 1994."]], "year": 1863},
	"wrld_1910_china_long_whisker_dragon": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "$1"], ["METAL", "manganese, nickel, copper"], ["FIRST OUT", "1794"], ["SOLD", "4 million"], ["MINTAGE", "300,000,000"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The dollar coin is a United States coin with a face value of one United States dollar."], ["MINTING", "C: Charlotte, North Carolina (gold coins only; 1838–1861). D: Dahlonega, Georgia (gold coins only; 1838–1861)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Only 15 silver dollars with the date of 1804 are known to exist; in 1999, one of them sold at auction for more than $4 million."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Succeeding base-metal $1 coins minted from 1971 onwards did not circulate widely as well, the most important reason being the continued circulation of the $1 bill."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The production of large numbers of U.S. gold coins (The first $1 and $20 gold coins were minted in 1849) from the new California mines lowered the price of gold, thereby increasing the value of silver."]], "year": 1794},
	"wrld_1911_china_chang_tso_lin_dolla": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "$1"], ["METAL", "manganese, nickel, copper"], ["FIRST OUT", "1794"], ["SOLD", "4 million"], ["MINTAGE", "300,000,000"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Despite efforts by the U.S. government to promote their use to save the cost of printing one dollar bills, the Anthony Dollar, the Sacagawea Dollar and the Presidential Dollar Series are all seldom seen in circulation, since most Americans prefer to use the dollar bill."], ["MINTING", "C: Charlotte, North Carolina (gold coins only; 1838–1861). D: Dahlonega, Georgia (gold coins only; 1838–1861)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Succeeding base-metal $1 coins minted from 1971 onwards did not circulate widely as well, the most important reason being the continued circulation of the $1 bill."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Since 1804 was the last recorded year of mintage for both the dollar and $10 Eagle, it was decided that the set would contain examples of those coins dated 1804, as well as the other denominations currently being produced."], ["ONE MORE THING", "By 1853, the value of a U.S. silver dollar contained in gold terms, $1.04 of silver, equal to $ 38.09 today."]], "year": 1794},
	"wrld_1732_mexico_pillar_8_reales": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "Hispanic Monarchy"], ["DIAMETER", "38 mm"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1497"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Because the Spanish dollar was widely used in Europe, the Americas, and the Far East, it became the first world currency by the 16th century."], ["MINTING", "The Coinage Act of 1792 specified that the U.S. dollar would contain 371.25 grains (24.057 g) pure or 416 grains (26.96 g) standard silver."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Spanish dollar, also known as the piece of eight (Spanish: real de a ocho, dólar, peso duro, peso fuerte or peso), is a silver coin of approximately 38 mm (1.5 in) diameter worth eight Spanish reales."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Most theories trace the origin of the \"$\" symbol, which originally had two vertical bars, to the pillars of Hercules wrapped in ribbons that appear on the reverse side of the Spanish dollar."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Following independence in 1821, Mexican coinage of silver reales and gold escudos followed that of Spanish lines until decimalization and the introduction of the peso worth 8 reales or 100 centavos."]], "year": 1497},
	"wrld_1652_massachusetts_pine_tree_s": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The pine tree shilling was a type of coin minted and circulated in the Thirteen Colonies. In 1652, the Massachusetts Bay Colony authorized Boston silversmiths John Hull and Robert Sanderson to mint coinage."], ["MINTING", "On May 27, 1652, the Massachusetts General Court appointed John Hull, a local silversmith, to be Boston's mint master without notifying or seeking permission from the British government."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Coins were issued in denominations of 3 and 6 pence and 1 shilling."]], "year": 0},
	"wrld_1776_continental_currency_doll": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "1 Continental dollar (not specified on coin)"], ["WEIGHT", "15–19 g"], ["DIAMETER", "≈38 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "6 mm"], ["METAL", "Pewter, brass, or silver"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1776"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Continental Currency dollar coin (also known as Continental dollar coin, Fugio dollar, or Franklino dollar) was the first pattern coin struck for the United States."], ["THE STORY", "While no legislation authorizing a dollar coin has been discovered, no resolutions from July 22, 1776, through September 26, 1778 mentioned the one-dollar banknote, suggesting that it was to have been replaced by a coin."], ["MINTING", "The 1787 Fugio cent, the first officially circulated coin of the United States, incorporated many elements of the design of the Continental Currency coin."], ["THE DESIGN", "An adaption of the Continental Currency dollar coin appears on the reverse of the \"Founding Father\" variety of the 2006 Benjamin Franklino silver dollar."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Only a few silver examples are known to exist."]], "year": 1776},
	"wrld_1808_napoleon_40_francs": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "First French Empire, Second French Empire"], ["FACE VALUE", "20"], ["DIAMETER", "21 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "1.3 mm"], ["METAL", "gold"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The denomination (known as the Franc Germinal) continued in use through the 19th century and later French gold coins in the same denomination were generally referred to as \"Napoléons\"."], ["THE STORY", "The 20 franc gold pieces which he authorized in 1803, became the model of all the coins of the Latin Monetary Union which circulated in Europe until 1914."], ["MINTING", "Over the years there were some twenty-three French mints not only in France but also in annexed or occupied countries, including Italy, Switzerland and the Netherlands."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Attempts were even taken to explore the unification of the European currency with the American dollar, which explains the extremely rare U.S. pattern coins carrying $4 marking on the face and 25 franc markings on the reverse."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The coins were originally minted in two denominations, 20 and 40 francs for Napoléon Bonaparte."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In only four years, from 1811 to 1815, he rose at the Royal Mint from a probationer to chief engraver."]], "year": 0},
	"wrld_1853_great_britain_gothic_crow": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["DIAMETER", "38 mm"], ["EDGE", "Milled"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1707–1981"], ["DESIGNER", "Joseph Booehm"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Always a heavy silver coin weighing around one ounce, during the 19th and 20th centuries the crown declined from being a real means of exchange to being a coin rarely spent, and minted for commemorative purposes only."], ["THE STORY", "Production of the Churchill crown began on 11 October 1965, and stopped in the summer of 1966. The 1927 \"wreath\" crowns were struck as proofs only (15,030 minted) and the 1934 coin had a mintage of just 932."], ["MINTING", "\"Decimal\" crowns were minted a few times after decimalisation of the British currency in 1971, initially with a nominal value of 25 (new) pence. The 1951 issue was for the Festival of Britain, and was only struck in proof condition."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The crown was a denomination of sterling coinage worth 1 ⁄ 4 of one pound, or 5 shillings, or 60 (old) pence. The crown coin was nicknamed the dollar, as one United States dollar on the gold standard was worth about the same (slightly less)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "From 1927 to 1939, the word \"CROWN\" appears, and from 1951 to 1960 this was changed to \"FIVE SHILLINGS\"."], ["ONE MORE THING", "For silver crowns, the grade of silver adhered to the long-standing standard (established in the 12th century by Henry II) – the Sterling Silver standard of 92.5% silver and 7.5% copper."]], "year": 1707},
	"wrld_1847_gothic_crown_victoria": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["DIAMETER", "38 mm"], ["EDGE", "Milled"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1707–1981"], ["DESIGNER", "Joseph Booehm"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The crown was a denomination of sterling coinage worth 1 ⁄ 4 of one pound, or 5 shillings, or 60 (old) pence."], ["THE STORY", "The 1953 crown was issued to celebrate the Coronation of Queen Elizabeth II, while the 1960 issue (which carried the same reverse design as the previous crown in 1953) commemorated the British Exhibition in New York."], ["MINTING", "The 1927 \"wreath\" crowns were struck as proofs only (15,030 minted) and the 1934 coin had a mintage of just 932. The coin was unique, issued as a pattern by engraver Thomas Simon in 1663 and nicknamed the \"Reddite Crown\"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Although all \"normal\" issues since 1951 have been composed of cupro-nickel, special proof versions have been produced for sale to collectors, and as gift items, in silver, gold, and occasionally platinum."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "After decimalisation on 15 February 1971, the 25-pence coin was introduced as a replacement for the crown as a commemorative coin."], ["ONE MORE THING", "From 1927 to 1939, the word \"CROWN\" appears, and from 1951 to 1960 this was changed to \"FIVE SHILLINGS\"."]], "year": 1707},
	"wrld_1741_austria_maria_theresa_tha": {"stats": [["YEARS STRUCK", "1741"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Maria Theresa thaler (MTT) is a silver bullion coin and a type of Conventionsthaler that has been used in world trade continuously since it was first minted in 1741."], ["THE STORY", "In 1741, the first MTT was struck according to the Reichsthaler standard with 1 ⁄ 9 of a Cologne mark of fine silver, or 25.98 grams."], ["MINTING", "Between 1751 and 2000, some 389 million were minted. The UK was the last government to agree formally to the request in February 1962."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Joseph Kalmer and Ludwig Hyun in the book Abessinien estimate that over 20% of 245 million coins minted until 1931 ended up in Ethiopia."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The MTT is first recorded as circulating in Ethiopia from Emperor Iyasu II of Ethiopia (1730–1755)."]], "year": 1741},
	"wrld_1966_mexico_hidalgo_10_pesos": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "Mexico"], ["FIRST OUT", "1863"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Modern peso and dollar currencies have a common origin in the 16th–19th century Spanish dollar, most continuing to use its sign, \"$\"."], ["THE STORY", "The post-independence silver peso contained 27.07 grams of 90.3% fine silver (24.44 g fine) while the gold peso or half escudo contained 1.6915 grams of 87.5% fine gold (1.48 g fine)."], ["MINTING", "Though the 50- and 100-peso coins are the only currently circulating coinage in the world to contain any silver, they rarely circulate because their silver content of 1/2 troy ounce have exceeded 100 pesos in value since around 2010."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On December 19, 2005, $100, $200, and $500 MXN banknotes in Series D1 were printed, including raised, tactile patterns, meant to make them distinguishable for people with vision incapacities."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The $200 was issued in 2008, and the $100 and $500 notes were released in August 2010."], ["ONE MORE THING", "After the Mexican peso crisis of December 1994, the peso sharply depreciated from MXN$3.4 to MXN$7.2 in 1995."]], "year": 1863},
	"wrld_1872_netherlands_ducat": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Its most familiar version, the gold ducat or sequin containing around 3.5 grams (0.11 troy ounces) of 98.6% fine gold, originated in Venice in 1284 and gained wide international acceptance over the centuries."], ["MINTING", "Germany and the Holy Roman Empire; many cities, states and principalities before 1871. The Hungarian mint still mints commemorative coins with 2, 3, 4 and 6-ducats quality."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The silver ducaton commenced in the Italian states in the mid-16th century as a large coin of approximately 30 grams fine silver, worth slightly less than the gold ducat or sequin."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Venetian ducat contained 3.545 grams of 99.47% fine gold, the highest purity medieval metallurgy could produce."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Continued depreciation in the silver currency during the 16th century, however, made the gold ducat worth more than 124 soldi."]], "year": 0},
	"wrld_1967_south_africa_krugerrand": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "South Africa"], ["FACE VALUE", "1 troy oz. fine gold"], ["WEIGHT", "33.93 g (1.09 troy oz)"], ["DIAMETER", "32.77 mm (1.28 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "2.84 mm (0.11 in)"], ["METAL", "Gold (91.67% Au, 8.33% Cu)"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1967–present"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "In 2017, the Rand Refinery began minting silver versions with the same overall design as the gold coin."], ["THE STORY", "Most of these sanctions were removed in 1991 after the South African government took steps to end its apartheid policies. Over 50 million ounces of gold Krugerrand coins have been sold since production started in 1967."], ["MINTING", "The South African Mint Company produces limited edition proof Krugerrands intended to be collectors' items rather than bullion investments."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Although gold Krugerrand coins have no face value, they are considered legal tender in South Africa by the South African Reserve Bank Act (SARBA) of 1989."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The image was designed by Coert Steynberg and was previously used on the reverse of the earlier South African five shillings (1947-51 and 1953-59) and 50 Cents (1961-64) coin."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The issue limit for these commemorative platinum, gold, and silver coins was 2,017 for platinum, 5,000 for gold, and 1,000,000 for silver."]], "year": 1967},
	"wrld_1979_canada_gold_maple_leaf": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "Canada"], ["FACE VALUE", "50.00 CAD (face value)"], ["WEIGHT", "31.11 g (1.00 troy oz)"], ["DIAMETER", "30 mm (1.181 in)"], ["EDGE", "Reeded"], ["METAL", "Gold (99.99% Au)"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1979–present"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Canadian Gold Maple Leaf (GML) is a gold bullion coin that is issued annually by the Government of Canada. It is produced by the Royal Canadian Mint."], ["MINTING", "At the time the only competing bullion coins being minted were the Krugerrand (which was not widely available because of the economic boycott of apartheid -era South Africa) and the Austrian 100 Corona."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Gold Maple Leaf is legal tender with a face value of 50 Canadian dollars."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The six-coin set was the first to include the 1 ⁄ 25 oz Maple Leaf denomination."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Having a.9999 millesimal fineness (24 karats), in some cases.99999, the coin is among the purest official bullion coins worldwide."]], "year": 1979},
	"wrld_1982_china_gold_panda": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "People's Republic of China"], ["EDGE", "Reeded"], ["METAL", "99.9% fine gold (Au)"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1982–present"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Chinese Gold Panda is a series of gold bullion coins issued by the People's Republic of China. The Official Mint of the People's Republic of China introduced the panda gold bullion coins in 1982."], ["THE STORY", "China issued its first gold coins picturing a panda design in 1982, in sizes of 1, 1 ⁄ 2, 1 ⁄ 4, and 1 ⁄ 10 ozt (31.1, 15.6, 7.8, and 3.1 g) of 99.9 fine gold. Beginning in 1983 another size was added – 1 ⁄ 20 ozt (1.6 g)."], ["MINTING", "An exception was 1991, when 1-gram Gold Pandas were minted, issued in the denomination of 3 yuan."], ["THE DESIGN", "Reverse: Different portraits of panda that changes every year (except for 2001 and 2002, which share the same design)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The panda design changes every year (with a single exception) and the Gold Panda coins come in different sizes and denominations, ranging from 1 ⁄ 20 to 1 troy ounce (1.6 to 31.1 grams) (and larger ones as well)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Larger panda coins were issued in some years, weighing 5 and 12 ozt (160 and 370 g)."]], "year": 1982},
	"wrld_1987_britannia_gold": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["FACE VALUE", "2 pounds sterling"], ["WEIGHT", "32.45 g, 31.21 g"], ["DIAMETER", "38.61 mm, 40 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "3 mm (2013–present day) mm"], ["EDGE", "Milled"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1997–present"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Gold Britannias also are issued in fractional sizes of one-half, one-quarter, and one-tenth of a troy ounce and with face values of £50, £25, and £10 respectively."], ["MINTING", "Britannia coins are British bullion coins issued by the Royal Mint in gold since 1987, in silver since 1997, and in platinum since 2018."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 2013 two additional sizes were introduced, a five-ounce coin of face value £500, and a fractional size of one-twentieth of face value £5. Britannia gold coins contain one troy ounce of gold and have a face value of £ 100."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "While mintage was limited prior to 2013, in 2013 and after mintage of the bullion version of the coin is unlimited based on demand."], ["ONE MORE THING", "From 2013 the gold coins have a millesimal fineness of 0.9999 (or 24 carat gold)."]], "year": 1997},
	"wrld_1989_australia_gold_nugget": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "Australia"], ["FACE VALUE", "Australian Dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "31.1 g"], ["DIAMETER", "32.1 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "2.8 mm"], ["EDGE", "Milled"], ["METAL", ".9999 gold"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Australian Gold Nugget, also sometimes known as the Australian Gold Kangaroo, is a gold bullion coin minted by the Perth Mint. The coins have been minted in denominations of 1 ⁄ 20 oz, 1 ⁄ 10 oz, 1 ⁄ 4 oz, 1 ⁄ 2 oz, 1 oz, 2 oz, 10 oz, and 1 kg of 24 carat gold."], ["THE STORY", "The face value of the coin is A$ 1 million, but at the time of minting it was valued at over A$ 53 million. The coin is approximately 80 cm (31 in) in diameter and 12 cm (4.7 in) thick."], ["MINTING", "In October 2011, the Perth Mint created a one tonne gold coin, breaking the record for the biggest and most valuable gold coin, previously held by the Royal Canadian Mint, the Big Maple Leaf."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Gold Nugget series was introduced in 1986 by Gold Corporation, a company wholly owned by the Government of Western Australia, more commonly known by its trading name The Perth Mint."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "For example, the Welcome Stranger nugget was depicted on the 1987 1 oz Australian Nugget coin while the 1 ⁄ 2 oz Australian Nugget coin of the same year showed the Hand of Faith nugget."]], "year": 1986},
	"wrld_1913_canada_gold_five_dollars": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "$1"], ["METAL", "manganese, nickel, copper"], ["FIRST OUT", "1794"], ["SOLD", "4 million"], ["MINTAGE", "300,000,000"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Dollar coins have been minted in the United States in gold, silver, and base metal versions."], ["MINTING", "C: Charlotte, North Carolina (gold coins only; 1838–1861). D: Dahlonega, Georgia (gold coins only; 1838–1861)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "A silver dollar would be worth $1 in silver if the price of silver is $1.29 per troy ounce. Only 15 silver dollars with the date of 1804 are known to exist; in 1999, one of them sold at auction for more than $4 million."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "By 1853, the value of a U.S. silver dollar contained in gold terms, $1.04 of silver, equal to $ 38.09 today."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In February 1887, all non-mutilated, non-chopmarked outstanding trade dollars were made redeemable to the United States Treasury for $1, and approximately 8 million of them were turned in."]], "year": 1794},
	"wrld_1890_victoria_jubilee_sovereig": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["FACE VALUE", "£1"], ["WEIGHT", "7.98805 g"], ["DIAMETER", "22.0 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "1.52 mm"], ["EDGE", "Milled (some not intended for circulation have plain edge)"], ["METAL", ".917 gold, .083 copper or other metals"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The sovereign is a British gold coin with a nominal value of one pound sterling (£1) and contains 0.2354 troy oz of pure gold."], ["THE STORY", "The new coin was struck in response to a large influx of gold into Europe from West Africa in the 1480s, and Henry at first called it the double ryal, but soon changed the name to sovereign."], ["MINTING", "The act which regulated currency in New South Wales came into force on 18 July 1855 and stipulated that the gold coins were to be called sovereigns and half sovereigns."], ["THE DESIGN", "Also removed in 1821 was the plume of hair, or streamer, behind George's helmet; it was restored in 1887, modified in 1893 and 1902, and eliminated in 2009."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The most recent special designs, in 2016 and 2017, were only for collectors. A 1953 sovereign sold at auction in 2014 for £384,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The California Gold Rush and other discoveries of the 1840s and 1850s boosted the amount of available gold and also the number of sovereigns struck, with £150 million in sovereigns and half sovereigns coined between 1850 and 1875."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Originally, the coin was unpopular because the public preferred the convenience of banknotes but paper currency of value £1 was soon limited by law."]], "year": 1817},
	"wrld_1911_george_v_sovereign": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["FACE VALUE", "£1"], ["WEIGHT", "7.98805 g"], ["DIAMETER", "22.0 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "1.52 mm"], ["EDGE", "Milled (some not intended for circulation have plain edge)"], ["METAL", ".917 gold, .083 copper or other metals"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The sovereign is a British gold coin with a nominal value of one pound sterling (£1) and contains 0.2354 troy oz of pure gold."], ["THE STORY", "The new coin was struck in response to a large influx of gold into Europe from West Africa in the 1480s, and Henry at first called it the double ryal, but soon changed the name to sovereign."], ["MINTING", "The 1932 sovereigns struck at Pretoria were the last to be issued intended as currency at their face value. Branch mints at Bombay (1918; mint mark \"I\") and Pretoria (1923–1932; mint mark \"SA\") also struck sovereigns."], ["THE DESIGN", "Also removed in 1821 was the plume of hair, or streamer, behind George's helmet; it was restored in 1887, modified in 1893 and 1902, and eliminated in 2009."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The 2017 collector's piece returned to Pistrucki's original design of 1817 for the modern sovereign's 200th birthday, with the Garter belt and motto. A 1953 sovereign sold at auction in 2014 for £384,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The California Gold Rush and other discoveries of the 1840s and 1850s boosted the amount of available gold and also the number of sovereigns struck, with £150 million in sovereigns and half sovereigns coined between 1850 and 1875."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Originally, the coin was unpopular because the public preferred the convenience of banknotes but paper currency of value £1 was soon limited by law."]], "year": 1817},
	"wrld_1915_austria_4_ducat_restrike": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Its most familiar version, the gold ducat or sequin containing around 3.5 grams (0.11 troy ounces) of 98.6% fine gold, originated in Venice in 1284 and gained wide international acceptance over the centuries."], ["MINTING", "Netherlands still issues golden and silver ducats having the same weight, composite and design when they were first minted in 1586. The Hungarian mint still mints commemorative coins with 2, 3, 4 and 6-ducats quality."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Continued depreciation in the silver currency during the 16th century, however, made the gold ducat worth more than 124 soldi. At that point a ducat worth 124 soldi emerged as a new silver-based unit of account for quoting salaries and costs."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The ducat had a variable price versus the silver Venetian lira, reaching 6.2 lire or 124 soldi (shillings) by 1470."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The ducat weighed 3.49 grams and was 23⅔ carats fine (3.442 g of pure gold) and exchanged at a ratio of 8 ducats for 11 Rhenish florins, which weighed 3.25 grams and were 18½ carats fine (2.503 g of pure gold)."]], "year": 0},
	"wrld_1947_mexico_50_pesos_centenari": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "0.01 U.S. Dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "2.5 g (0.08 troy oz)"], ["DIAMETER", "19.05 mm (0.75 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "1.52 mm (0.0598 in)"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["METAL", "copper -plated zinc 97.5% Zn, 2.5% Cu"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The penny is issued in its current form as the Lincoln cent, with its obverse featuring the profile of President Abraham Lincoln since 1909, the centennial of his birth."], ["THE STORY", "The isotope composition of early coins spanning the period 1828 to 1843 reflects the copper from Cornish ores from England, while coins after 1850 reflect the Keweenaw Peninsula, Michigan ores, a finding consistent with historical records."], ["MINTING", "The only significant divergence is that the small 3 was non-descending (the same size as a 0, 1, or 2) in the early history, before switching to a descending, large 3 for the year 1934 and then permanently (as of 2014) in 1943."], ["THE DESIGN", "Throughout its history, the Lincoln cent has featured several typefaces for the date, but most of the digits have been old-style numerals, except with the 4 and 8 neither ascending nor descending."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Presidential $1 Coin Act of 2005 required that the cent's reverse be redesigned in 2009. For example, the loss in 2013 was $55 million."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "When the Lincoln one-cent coin made its initial appearance in 1909, it marked a radical departure from the accepted styling of United States coinage, as it was the first regular coin to bear a portrait other than the mythical Liberty which appeared on most pre-1909 regular coins."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Aluminum was chosen, and over 1.5 million samples of the 1974 aluminum cent were struck before ultimately being rejected."]], "year": 1793},
	"wrld_1897_russia_nicholas_ii_15_rou": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "Russia"], ["UNIT", "ruble"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The ruble is subdivided into 100 kopecks."], ["THE STORY", "Initially an uncoined unit of account, the ruble became a circulating coin in 1704 just before the establishment of the Russian Empire."], ["MINTING", "The 1 and 5-ruble coins were minted in brass-clad steel, the 10 and 20-ruble coins in cupro-nickel, and the 50 and 100-ruble coins were bimetallic (aluminium-bronze and cupro-nickel-zinc)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "It is nearly identical to those of the 5- Swiss franc coin (31.45 mm, 13.2 g cupronickel), worth approx. €4.39 or US$5.09 as of August 2018."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "On 23 March 2022, President Putin announced that Russia would only accept payments for Russian gas exports from “unfriendly countries” in rubles."], ["ONE MORE THING", "After the fall of the Soviet Union, the Russian Federation introduced new coins in 1992 in denominations of 1, 5, 10, 20, 50, and 100 rubles."]], "year": 0},
	"wrld_1923_mexico_gold_dos_pesos": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "Mexico"], ["FIRST OUT", "1863"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Modern peso and dollar currencies have a common origin in the 16th–19th century Spanish dollar, most continuing to use its sign, \"$\"."], ["THE STORY", "The post-independence silver peso contained 27.07 grams of 90.3% fine silver (24.44 g fine) while the gold peso or half escudo contained 1.6915 grams of 87.5% fine gold (1.48 g fine)."], ["MINTING", "In 1882, cupro-nickel 1, 2 and 5 centavos coins were issued but they were only minted for two years. From 1918 onward the weight and fineness of all the silver coins declined, until 1979, when the last silver 100-peso coins were minted."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The $200 was issued in 2008, and the $100 and $500 notes were released in August 2010. As of 2020, the coins most commonly encountered in circulation have face values of 50¢, $1, $2, $5 and $10."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "A new $1000 note was issued on November 15, 2004, which was worth about US$88 upon introduction."], ["ONE MORE THING", "On December 19, 2005, $100, $200, and $500 MXN banknotes in Series D1 were printed, including raised, tactile patterns, meant to make them distinguishable for people with vision incapacities."]], "year": 1863},
	"wrld_1898_mexico_cap_and_rays_8_rea": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "Hispanic Monarchy"], ["DIAMETER", "38 mm"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1497"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Most theories trace the origin of the \"$\" symbol, which originally had two vertical bars, to the pillars of Hercules wrapped in ribbons that appear on the reverse side of the Spanish dollar."], ["MINTING", "Following independence in 1821, Mexican coinage of silver reales and gold escudos followed that of Spanish lines until decimalization and the introduction of the peso worth 8 reales or 100 centavos."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Spanish dollar, also known as the piece of eight (Spanish: real de a ocho, dólar, peso duro, peso fuerte or peso), is a silver coin of approximately 38 mm (1.5 in) diameter worth eight Spanish reales."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 1813, Governor Lachlan Macquarie made creative use of £10,000 in Spanish dollars sent by the British government."], ["ONE MORE THING", "More important, however, was that the Spanish dollar, from the 16th to the 19th century, was relatively the most stable and least debased coin in the Western world."]], "year": 1497},
	"wrld_1890_great_britain_victoria_cr": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["DIAMETER", "38 mm"], ["EDGE", "Milled"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1707–1981"], ["DESIGNER", "Joseph Booehm"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The crown was a denomination of sterling coinage worth 1 ⁄ 4 of one pound, or 5 shillings, or 60 (old) pence."], ["THE STORY", "In 2014, a new world record price was achieved for a milled silver crown. The 1927 \"wreath\" crowns were struck as proofs only (15,030 minted) and the 1934 coin had a mintage of just 932."], ["MINTING", "The 1951 issue was for the Festival of Britain, and was only struck in proof condition. The coin was unique, issued as a pattern by engraver Thomas Simon in 1663 and nicknamed the \"Reddite Crown\"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Most Commonwealth countries still issue crown-sized coins for sale to collectors. The crown coin was nicknamed the dollar, as one United States dollar on the gold standard was worth about the same (slightly less)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "From 1927 to 1939, the word \"CROWN\" appears, and from 1951 to 1960 this was changed to \"FIVE SHILLINGS\"."], ["ONE MORE THING", "For silver crowns, the grade of silver adhered to the long-standing standard (established in the 12th century by Henry II) – the Sterling Silver standard of 92.5% silver and 7.5% copper."]], "year": 1707},
	"wrld_1935_great_britain_george_v_ju": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["DIAMETER", "38 mm"], ["EDGE", "Milled"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1707–1981"], ["DESIGNER", "Joseph Booehm"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Always a heavy silver coin weighing around one ounce, during the 19th and 20th centuries the crown declined from being a real means of exchange to being a coin rarely spent, and minted for commemorative purposes only."], ["THE STORY", "The coin was unique, issued as a pattern by engraver Thomas Simon in 1663 and nicknamed the \"Reddite Crown\". The 1927 \"wreath\" crowns were struck as proofs only (15,030 minted) and the 1934 coin had a mintage of just 932."], ["MINTING", "\"Decimal\" crowns were minted a few times after decimalisation of the British currency in 1971, initially with a nominal value of 25 (new) pence. The 1951 issue was for the Festival of Britain, and was only struck in proof condition."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The crown coin was nicknamed the dollar, as one United States dollar on the gold standard was worth about the same (slightly less). The crown was a denomination of sterling coinage worth 1 ⁄ 4 of one pound, or 5 shillings, or 60 (old) pence."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "After decimalisation on 15 February 1971, the 25-pence coin was introduced as a replacement for the crown as a commemorative coin."], ["ONE MORE THING", "From 1927 to 1939, the word \"CROWN\" appears, and from 1951 to 1960 this was changed to \"FIVE SHILLINGS\"."]], "year": 1707},
	"wrld_1938_canada_silver_dollar": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "Canada"], ["FIRST OUT", "1935"], ["BIG SALE", "$160,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Canadian silver dollar (French: Dollar argent du Canada) was first issued by the Royal Canadian Mint in 1935 to commemorate the Silver Jubilee of King George V."], ["THE STORY", "In December 1955, the Royal Canadian Mint made up an order of 2,000 silver dollars for a firm in Arnprior, Ontario."], ["MINTING", "One of the silver coins, owned by the Royal Mint Museum, has been loaned to the Bank of Canada since 1976 and has been displayed in the Bank of Canada Museum since 1980."], ["THE DESIGN", "An element that was common in every denomination of 1953 was the two obverses that existed."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The coin changed hands several more times, being sold to Anthony Carrato of Eagle Coin Company for US$160,000 in July 1979, then Joseph Carlton and David Hirschman of Carlton Numismatics Inc. for US$325,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The very low mintages of these made the 1947 Maple Leaf and 1948 dollars exceptionally rare, with the 1948 Silver Dollar commanding a very high market price."], ["ONE MORE THING", "After his death in 2018, Cook's collection was auctioned off during the American Numismatic Association World's Fair of Money in Chicago, where it was bought one final time by Sandy Campbell and Ian Laing for $734,000 after a 20% buyer's fee."]], "year": 1935},
	"wrld_1953_coronation_crown": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["DIAMETER", "38 mm"], ["EDGE", "Milled"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1707–1981"], ["DESIGNER", "Joseph Booehm"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The crown was a denomination of sterling coinage worth 1 ⁄ 4 of one pound, or 5 shillings, or 60 (old) pence."], ["THE STORY", "The coin was unique, issued as a pattern by engraver Thomas Simon in 1663 and nicknamed the \"Reddite Crown\". The 1927 \"wreath\" crowns were struck as proofs only (15,030 minted) and the 1934 coin had a mintage of just 932."], ["MINTING", "In 1853, the Royal Mint had produced two patterns for a gold 5-shilling coin for circulation use, one denominated as five shillings and the other as a quarter sovereign, but this coin never went into production, in part due to concerns about the small size of the coin and likely wear in circulation."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Although all \"normal\" issues since 1951 have been composed of cupro-nickel, special proof versions have been produced for sale to collectors, and as gift items, in silver, gold, and occasionally platinum."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Always a heavy silver coin weighing around one ounce, during the 19th and 20th centuries the crown declined from being a real means of exchange to being a coin rarely spent, and minted for commemorative purposes only."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The crown coin was nicknamed the dollar, as one United States dollar on the gold standard was worth about the same (slightly less)."]], "year": 1707},
	"wrld_1967_canada_centennial_silver": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "$1"], ["METAL", "manganese, nickel, copper"], ["FIRST OUT", "1794"], ["SOLD", "4 million"], ["MINTAGE", "300,000,000"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Dollar coins were first minted in the United States in 1794."], ["MINTING", "O: New Orleans, Louisiana (1838–1861; 1879–1909). C: Charlotte, North Carolina (gold coins only; 1838–1861)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In February 1887, all non-mutilated, non-chopmarked outstanding trade dollars were made redeemable to the United States Treasury for $1, and approximately 8 million of them were turned in."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The current silver price (January 29, 2021) is $27.03 per troy ounce so a silver dollar is worth, in melt value of about US$20.90."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 2019, the GAO re-estimated the cost of replacing the $1 bill and found for the first time that it would cause the government to lose between $611 million and $2.6 billion because physical money was being used less, resulting in dollar bills remaining in circulation longer compared to the 2011 analysis."]], "year": 1794},
	"wrld_1969_isle_of_man_crown": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["DIAMETER", "38 mm"], ["EDGE", "Milled"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1707–1981"], ["DESIGNER", "Joseph Booehm"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The crown was a denomination of sterling coinage worth 1 ⁄ 4 of one pound, or 5 shillings, or 60 (old) pence."], ["THE STORY", "The 1951 issue was for the Festival of Britain, and was only struck in proof condition. The 1927 \"wreath\" crowns were struck as proofs only (15,030 minted) and the 1934 coin had a mintage of just 932."], ["MINTING", "Always a heavy silver coin weighing around one ounce, during the 19th and 20th centuries the crown declined from being a real means of exchange to being a coin rarely spent, and minted for commemorative purposes only."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Most Commonwealth countries still issue crown-sized coins for sale to collectors. The crown coin was nicknamed the dollar, as one United States dollar on the gold standard was worth about the same (slightly less)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The 1953 crown was issued to celebrate the Coronation of Queen Elizabeth II, while the 1960 issue (which carried the same reverse design as the previous crown in 1953) commemorated the British Exhibition in New York."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Production of the Churchill crown began on 11 October 1965, and stopped in the summer of 1966."]], "year": 1707},
	"anct_27_bc_augustus_aureus": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "Roman Republic"], ["METAL", "gold"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The aureus was about the same size as the denarius, but heavier (since gold has a higher density than silver)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The aureus (pl. aurei, 'golden', used as a noun) was a gold coin of ancient Rome originally valued at 25 pure silver denarii (sin. denarius)."]], "year": 0},
	"anct_68_ad_nero_aureus": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "Roman Republic"], ["METAL", "gold"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Before the time of Julius Caesar the aureus was struck infrequently."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The aureus (pl. aurei, 'golden', used as a noun) was a gold coin of ancient Rome originally valued at 25 pure silver denarii (sin. denarius)."]], "year": 0},
	"anct_1252_florence_gold_florin": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["FACE VALUE", "2 shillings"], ["WEIGHT", "11.31 g"], ["DIAMETER", "28.0 mm (1851–1886) 30.0 mm (1887–1892) 29.5 mm.."], ["EDGE", "Milled"], ["METAL", "92.5% Ag (1920–1946) 50% Ag (1947–1970) Cupronickel"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1849–1970"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "In 1911, following the accession of George V, the florin regained the shields and sceptres design it had in the late Victorian era, and it kept that motif until 1937, when the national emblems were placed on it."], ["THE STORY", "In 1847, a motion was introduced in Parliament by Sir John Bowring calling for the introduction of a decimal currency and the striking of coins of one-tenth and one-hundredth of a pound."], ["MINTING", "The Jubilee florin was struck each year between 1887 and 1892. Florins bearing a left-facing effigy of George V by Sir Bertram Mackennal were minted in each year of the King's reign (1910–1936) except 1910 and 1934."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The British florin, or two- shilling piece (2/– or 2 s.), was a coin worth 1 ⁄ 10 of one pound, or 24 pence."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "To better distinguish it from the half crown, the diameter was reduced from 29 to 28.5 millimetres (1.14 to 1.12 in)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "From 1947, the metal content was changed, as for all British silver circulating coins, to 75% copper, 25% nickel."]], "year": 1849},
	"anct_1284_venice_gold_ducat": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Its most familiar version, the gold ducat or sequin containing around 3.5 grams (0.11 troy ounces) of 98.6% fine gold, originated in Venice in 1284 and gained wide international acceptance over the centuries."], ["MINTING", "Before the Swiss unification, the Swiss also minted ducats, the most well known of which are the Zürich ducats. The Hungarian mint still mints commemorative coins with 2, 3, 4 and 6-ducats quality."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The rarity of ducats that Genoese traders struck at Mytilene, Phocaea, and Pera suggests that Venetians melted those they encountered. At that point a ducat worth 124 soldi emerged as a new silver-based unit of account for quoting salaries and costs."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Ladislaus carried on the Hungarian prototype, but naming him with a different legend."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In the 19th century ducats were progressively dropped as standard coin of several nations, most significantly the Latin Monetary Union of 1865 (France, Italy, Switzerland) and the Vienna Monetary Treaty of 1857 (German Confederation, Austria-Hungary)."]], "year": 0},
	"anct_330_constantine_the_great_soli": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "He played a pivotal role in elevating the status of Christianity in Rome, decriminalizing Christian practice and ceasing Christian persecution in a period referred to as the Constantinian shift."], ["THE STORY", "Although Constantine's death follows the conclusion of the Persian campaign in Eusebius's account, most other sources report his death as occurring in its middle."], ["MINTING", "The new system did not last long: Constantine refused to accept the demotion and continued to style himself as augustus on his coinage, even as other members of the Tetrarchy referred to him as a caesar on theirs."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "More significantly, in 325 he summoned the First Council of Nicaea, most known for its dealing with Arianism and for instituting the Nicene Creed."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Constantine died soon after at a suburban villa called Achyron, on the last day of the fifty-day festival of Pentecost directly following Pascha (or Easter), on 22 May 337."], ["ONE MORE THING", "By 336, he had reoccupied most of the long-lost province of Dacia which Aurelian had been forced to abandon in 271."]], "year": 0},
	"anct_1066_william_the_conqueror_pen": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "0.01 U.S. Dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "2.5 g (0.08 troy oz)"], ["DIAMETER", "19.05 mm (0.75 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "1.52 mm (0.0598 in)"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["METAL", "copper -plated zinc 97.5% Zn, 2.5% Cu"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "From 1959 (the sesquicentennial of Lincoln's birth) to 2008, the reverse featured the Lincoln Memorial."], ["THE STORY", "Some 1982 cents used the 97.5% zinc composition, while others used the 95% copper composition. Aluminum was chosen, and over 1.5 million samples of the 1974 aluminum cent were struck before ultimately being rejected."], ["MINTING", "Similarly, the digit 5 was small and non-descending up to 1945."], ["THE DESIGN", "Throughout its history, the Lincoln cent has featured several typefaces for the date, but most of the digits have been old-style numerals, except with the 4 and 8 neither ascending nor descending."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Presidential $1 Coin Act of 2005 required that the cent's reverse be redesigned in 2009. For example, the loss in 2013 was $55 million."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "When the Lincoln one-cent coin made its initial appearance in 1909, it marked a radical departure from the accepted styling of United States coinage, as it was the first regular coin to bear a portrait other than the mythical Liberty which appeared on most pre-1909 regular coins."], ["ONE MORE THING", "While the smallest mintage is the '09-S Indian, the '09-S VDB is the key Lincoln date, and hence is most valuable."]], "year": 1793},
	"anct_696_umayyad_gold_dinar": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "Arab Caliphate, Umayyad Caliphate"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "0696"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The gold dinar (Arabic: ﺩﻳﻨﺎﺭ ذهب) is an Islamic medieval gold coin first issued in AH 77 (696–697 CE) by Caliph Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan. The weight of the dinar is 1 mithqal (4.25 grams or 0.137 troy ounces)."], ["THE STORY", "These corresponded in weight to only 20 carats (4.0 g) rather than the 24 carats of a recently minted solidus, but matched with the weight of the worn solidi that were circulating in those areas at the time."], ["MINTING", "The average weight of sixty undamaged specimens of AH 79–84 (698–704 CE) is only 2.71 grams, a figure very close to that for a unique coin of (AH 79) 698 struck with no mint name (as was the standard procedure for the gold dinars produced in Damascus)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The gold coins were first struck to the contemporary standard of 4.4 grams and with one or more Arabic Standing figures on the obverse and an Arabic legend on the reverse."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The marginal legend states: \"Muhammad is the Messenger of God, he was sent with guidance and the religion of truth to make it prevail over every other religion, averse though the idolaters may be\" (Quran 9:33)."]], "year": 0},
	"anct_120_hadrian_aureus": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "Roman Republic"], ["METAL", "gold"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "At about the same time the purity of the silver coinage was also slightly decreased."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The aureus (pl. aurei, 'golden', used as a noun) was a gold coin of ancient Rome originally valued at 25 pure silver denarii (sin. denarius)."]], "year": 0},
	"anct_340_bc_ephesus_bee_tetradrachm": {"stats": [["FACE VALUE", "4"], ["WEIGHT", "17 g"], ["METAL", "silver"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "-0600-01-01T00:00:00Z"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "As a result, tetradrachms were minted in vast quantities by various polities in many weight and fineness standards, though the Athens -derived Attic standard of about 17.2 grams was the most common."], ["MINTING", "To differentiate their currency from the rival coinage of Aegina using the Aeginetic stater of about 12.3 grams, Athens minted its tetradrachm based on the \"Attic\" standard of 4.3 grams per drachma."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Due to their often high artistic level tetradrachms are eagerly collected in modern times, and well-preserved or rare specimens can reach considerable prices."]], "year": 0},
	"anct_300_bc_ptolemy_i_tetradrachm": {"stats": [["FACE VALUE", "4"], ["WEIGHT", "17 g"], ["METAL", "silver"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "-0600-01-01T00:00:00Z"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Because of their large size, tetradrachms were often used by various states or rulers to advertise themselves or to deliver political messages."], ["MINTING", "As a result, tetradrachms were minted in vast quantities by various polities in many weight and fineness standards, though the Athens -derived Attic standard of about 17.2 grams was the most common."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Due to their often high artistic level tetradrachms are eagerly collected in modern times, and well-preserved or rare specimens can reach considerable prices."]], "year": 0},
	"anct_160_marcus_aurelius_denarius": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "Ancient Rome"], ["METAL", "silver"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The denarius (Latin:; pl.: dēnāriī, Latin:) was the standard Roman silver coin from its introduction in the Second Punic War c."], ["THE STORY", "A predecessor of the denarius was first struck in 269 or 268 BC, five years before the First Punic War, with an average weight of 6.81 grams, or 1 ⁄ 48 of a Roman pound."], ["MINTING", "It continued to be minted in very small quantities, likely for ceremonial purposes, until and through the Tetrarchy (293–313)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Using the cost of bread as a baseline, this pay equates to around US$20 in 2013 terms. Expressed in terms of the price of silver, and assuming 0.999 purity, a 1 ⁄ 10 troy ounce denarius had a precious metal value of around US$2.60 in 2021."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The denarius contained an average 4.5 grams, or 1 ⁄ 72 of a Roman pound, of silver, and was at first tariffed at ten asses, hence its name, which means 'tenner'."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Under Julius Caesar, this was doubled to 225 denarii /yr, with soldiers having to pay for their own food and arms, while in the reign of Augustus a Centurion received at least 3,750 denarii per year, and for the highest rank, 15,000 denarii."]], "year": 0},
	"anct_440_bc_athens_owl_tetradrachm": {"stats": [["FACE VALUE", "4"], ["WEIGHT", "17 g"], ["METAL", "silver"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "-0600-01-01T00:00:00Z"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "As a result, tetradrachms were minted in vast quantities by various polities in many weight and fineness standards, though the Athens -derived Attic standard of about 17.2 grams was the most common."], ["MINTING", "To differentiate their currency from the rival coinage of Aegina using the Aeginetic stater of about 12.3 grams, Athens minted its tetradrachm based on the \"Attic\" standard of 4.3 grams per drachma."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Due to their often high artistic level tetradrachms are eagerly collected in modern times, and well-preserved or rare specimens can reach considerable prices."]], "year": 0},
