	"anct_211_bc_roman_republic_denarius": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "Ancient Rome"], ["METAL", "silver"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The denarius (Latin:; pl.: dēnāriī, Latin:) was the standard Roman silver coin from its introduction in the Second Punic War c."], ["THE STORY", "The denarius contained an average 4.5 grams, or 1 ⁄ 72 of a Roman pound, of silver, and was at first tariffed at ten asses, hence its name, which means 'tenner'."], ["MINTING", "A predecessor of the denarius was first struck in 269 or 268 BC, five years before the First Punic War, with an average weight of 6.81 grams, or 1 ⁄ 48 of a Roman pound."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Expressed in terms of the price of silver, and assuming 0.999 purity, a 1 ⁄ 10 troy ounce denarius had a precious metal value of around US$2.60 in 2021. Using the cost of bread as a baseline, this pay equates to around US$20 in 2013 terms."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Under the rule of Augustus (27 BC – 14 AD) its weight fell to 3.9 grams (a theoretical weight of 1 ⁄ 84 of a Roman pound)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "During the republic (509 BC – 27 BC), a legionary earned 112.5 denarii per year (0.3 denarii per day)."]], "year": 0},
	"anct_14_ad_tiberius_tribute_penny": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "0.01 U.S. Dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "2.5 g (0.08 troy oz)"], ["DIAMETER", "19.05 mm (0.75 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "1.52 mm (0.0598 in)"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["METAL", "copper -plated zinc 97.5% Zn, 2.5% Cu"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "It has been the lowest face-value physical unit of U.S. currency since the abolition of the half-cent in 1857 (the abstract mill, which has never been minted, equal to a tenth of a cent, continues to see limited use in the fields of taxation and finance)."], ["THE STORY", "From 1944 to 1946, salvaged ammunition shells made their way into the minting process, and it was not uncommon to see coins featuring streaks of brass or having a considerably darker finish than other issues."], ["MINTING", "The only significant divergence is that the small 3 was non-descending (the same size as a 0, 1, or 2) in the early history, before switching to a descending, large 3 for the year 1934 and then permanently (as of 2014) in 1943."], ["THE DESIGN", "Throughout its history, the Lincoln cent has featured several typefaces for the date, but most of the digits have been old-style numerals, except with the 4 and 8 neither ascending nor descending."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Special 2009 cents struck for sale in sets to collectors had the metallic copper content of cents minted in 1909 (95% copper, 5% tin and zinc). The Presidential $1 Coin Act of 2005 required that the cent's reverse be redesigned in 2009."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "As of January 21, 2014, a pre-1982 cent contained 2.203 cents' worth of copper and zinc, making it an attractive target for melting by people wanting to sell the metals for profit."], ["ONE MORE THING", "For example, the loss in 2013 was $55 million."]], "year": 1793},
	"anct_1279_edward_i_penny": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FACE VALUE", "0.01 U.S. Dollar"], ["WEIGHT", "2.5 g (0.08 troy oz)"], ["DIAMETER", "19.05 mm (0.75 in)"], ["THICKNESS", "1.52 mm (0.0598 in)"], ["EDGE", "Plain"], ["METAL", "copper -plated zinc 97.5% Zn, 2.5% Cu"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The penny is issued in its current form as the Lincoln cent, with its obverse featuring the profile of President Abraham Lincoln since 1909, the centennial of his birth."], ["THE STORY", "Some 1982 cents used the 97.5% zinc composition, while others used the 95% copper composition. Aluminum was chosen, and over 1.5 million samples of the 1974 aluminum cent were struck before ultimately being rejected."], ["MINTING", "Similarly, the digit 5 was small and non-descending up to 1945."], ["THE DESIGN", "Throughout its history, the Lincoln cent has featured several typefaces for the date, but most of the digits have been old-style numerals, except with the 4 and 8 neither ascending nor descending."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Violators can be punished with a fine of up to $10,000 USD, imprisoned for a maximum of five years, or both. The Presidential $1 Coin Act of 2005 required that the cent's reverse be redesigned in 2009."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "When the Lincoln one-cent coin made its initial appearance in 1909, it marked a radical departure from the accepted styling of United States coinage, as it was the first regular coin to bear a portrait other than the mythical Liberty which appeared on most pre-1909 regular coins."], ["ONE MORE THING", "While the smallest mintage is the '09-S Indian, the '09-S VDB is the key Lincoln date, and hence is most valuable."]], "year": 1793},
	"anct_1489_henry_vii_sovereign": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["FACE VALUE", "£1"], ["WEIGHT", "7.98805 g"], ["DIAMETER", "22.0 mm"], ["THICKNESS", "1.52 mm"], ["EDGE", "Milled (some not intended for circulation have plain edge)"], ["METAL", ".917 gold, .083 copper or other metals"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The coin was named after the English gold sovereign, which was last minted about 1603, and originated as part of the Great Recoinage of 1816."], ["THE STORY", "In the 1660s, following the Restoration of Charles II and the mechanisation of the Royal Mint that quickly followed, a new twenty-shilling gold coin was issued."], ["MINTING", "When Pistrucki created the coin, Neoclassicism was all the rage in London, and he may have been inspired by the Elgin Marbles, which were exhibited from 1807, and which he probably saw soon after his arrival in London."], ["THE DESIGN", "Pistrucki's sovereign was unusual for a British coin of the 19th century in not having a heraldic design, but this was consistent with Pole's desire to make the sovereign look as different from the guinea as possible."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The 2017 collector's piece returned to Pistrucki's original design of 1817 for the modern sovereign's 200th birthday, with the Garter belt and motto. A 1953 sovereign sold at auction in 2014 for £384,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "As Britain moved towards war in the July Crisis of 1914, many sought to convert Bank of England notes into gold, and the bank's reserves of the metal fell from £27 million on 29 July to £11 million on 1 August."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Extremely rare, one sold in 2020 for £1,000,000, setting what was then a record (since broken) for a British coin."]], "year": 1817},
	"anct_1351_edward_iii_noble": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The noble was the first English gold coin produced in quantity, introduced during the second coinage (1344–1346) of King Edward III."], ["THE STORY", "Transitional period (1361) and Treaty period (1361–1369) (obverse): EDWARD DEI GRA REX ANGL DNS HYB Z ACQ (Edward by the grace of God King of England Lord of Ireland and Aquitaine)."], ["MINTING", "Nobles produced during the reign of King Henry IV (1399–1413) are divided into the \"Heavy Coinage\" of 120 grains (7.8 grams) produced until 1412, and the \"Light coinage\" of 108 grains (7.0 grams) produced in 1412–13."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The price of gold rose from the 1430s onward, so gold coins were worth more in Europe than in England, which resulted in a gold shortage in England as coins were exported for profit."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Nobles were struck throughout Henry VI's first reign (1422–61), but a shortage of gold resulted in fewer coins being struck."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Only a small quantity of nobles were minted during Edward IV's Heavy Coinage period (1461–64), at London."]], "year": 0},
	"anct_1470_edward_iv_angel": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "In 1472, the half-angel was introduced with a similar design weighing 40 grains (2.6 grammes) with a diameter of 20 to 21 millimetres."], ["MINTING", "It was last minted during the reign of Charles I in 1642 before the English Civil War (1642–1651)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The angel varied in value from 6 shillings 8 pence to 11 shillings between Edward's reign and the time of James I."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 1550 during the reign of Edward VI it increased to ten shillings (10/-) or 120 pence or £ 1 ⁄ 2."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 1544, it increased again to eight shillings (8/-) or 96 pence."]], "year": 0},
