	"anct_1550_spanish_cob_2_reales": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "Hispanic Monarchy"], ["DIAMETER", "38 mm"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1497"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The Spanish dollar, also known as the piece of eight (Spanish: real de a ocho, dólar, peso duro, peso fuerte or peso), is a silver coin of approximately 38 mm (1.5 in) diameter worth eight Spanish reales."], ["MINTING", "The Spanish dollar was the coin upon which the original United States dollar was based (at 0.7735 troy ounces or 24.06 grams), and it remained legal tender in the United States until the Coinage Act of 1857."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Following independence in 1821, Mexican coinage of silver reales and gold escudos followed that of Spanish lines until decimalization and the introduction of the peso worth 8 reales or 100 centavos."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Most theories trace the origin of the \"$\" symbol, which originally had two vertical bars, to the pillars of Hercules wrapped in ribbons that appear on the reverse side of the Spanish dollar."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The pricing of equities on U.S. stock exchanges in 1 ⁄ 8 -dollar denominations persisted until the New York Stock Exchange converted first to pricing in sixteenths of a dollar on 24 June 1997, and then in 2001 to decimal pricing."]], "year": 1497},
	"anct_117_trajan_sestertius": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "Ancient Rome"], ["WEIGHT", "27.3 g, 1.1 g"], ["METAL", "silver, bronze, brass"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "-0210-01-01T00:00:00Z"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "During the Roman Republic it was a small, silver coin issued only on rare occasions."], ["THE STORY", "By the 260s and 270s the main unit was the double-denarius, the Antoninianus, but by then these small coins were almost all bronze. Some of the last sestertii were struck by Aurelian (270–275 AD)."], ["MINTING", "Most were struck in the mint of Rome but from AD 64 during the reign of Nero (AD 54–68) and Vespasian (AD 69–79), the mint of Lyon (Lugdunum), supplemented production."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Although these coins were theoretically worth eight sestertii, the average sestertius was worth far more in plain terms of the metal it contained. Records from Pompeii show a slave being sold at auction for 6,252 sestertii."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The sestertius was produced as the largest brass denomination until the late 3rd century AD."]], "year": 0},
	"anct_250_bc_seleucid_tetradrachm": {"stats": [["FACE VALUE", "4"], ["WEIGHT", "17 g"], ["METAL", "silver"], ["YEARS STRUCK", "-0600-01-01T00:00:00Z"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Because of their large size, tetradrachms were often used by various states or rulers to advertise themselves or to deliver political messages."], ["MINTING", "To differentiate their currency from the rival coinage of Aegina using the Aeginetic stater of about 12.3 grams, Athens minted its tetradrachm based on the \"Attic\" standard of 4.3 grams per drachma."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Due to their often high artistic level tetradrachms are eagerly collected in modern times, and well-preserved or rare specimens can reach considerable prices."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "As a result, tetradrachms were minted in vast quantities by various polities in many weight and fineness standards, though the Athens -derived Attic standard of about 17.2 grams was the most common."]], "year": 0},
	"anct_1600_elizabeth_i_sixpence": {"stats": [["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["FACE VALUE", "£0.025 6 d (until 1971) 2 1 ⁄ 2 p (1971–1980)"], ["WEIGHT", "2.83 g"], ["DIAMETER", "19.41 mm"], ["EDGE", "Milled"], ["METAL", "Silver (1816–1920) 92.5% Ag (1920–1946) 50% Ag.."], ["YEARS STRUCK", "1551–1970"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Before Decimal Day in 1971, sterling used the Carolingian monetary system (£sd), under which the largest unit was a pound (£), divisible into 20 shillings (s), each worth 12 pence (d), the value of two pre-decimal sixpence coins."], ["THE STORY", "Hence, newly minted sixpences weighed 1 ⁄ 11 troy ounce, equivalent to 43.636 grains or 2.828 grams. The Royal Mint debased the silver coinage in 1920 from 92.5% silver to 50% silver."], ["MINTING", "Although of higher quality than hammered coins, Mestrelle's sixpences were more expensive to produce, and machine-struck coinage ceased to be minted in 1572."], ["THE DESIGN", "Unusually, the sixpences minted in 1561 and 1562 were milled, i.e. produced by machine rather than by hand, with the press of the Frenchman Eloy Mestrelle, who had been granted authority to mint coins by the queen."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 2016, new decimal sixpences (face value £0.06) began being minted by the Royal Mint as commemorative issues; these coins have been produced for each year since then, and are minted in sterling silver."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Sixpences continued to be legal tender, with a value of 2 + 1 ⁄ 2 new pence, until 30 June 1980."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The first sixpences were minted in 1551, during the reign of Edward VI."]], "year": 1551},
	"anct_1820_chinese_cash_coin": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Generally most cash coins were made from copper or bronze alloys, with iron, lead, and zinc coins occasionally used less often throughout Chinese history."], ["THE STORY", "After the year 621, cash coins typically had 4 character obverse Inscriptions consisting of \"bao\", reading as \"寳\"."], ["MINTING", "Another effect of the contemporary copper shortages was that the Qing government started importing Korean 5 fun coins and overstruck them with \"10 cash\"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "At the introduction of red cash system in Southern Xinjiang in 1760, the exchange rate of standard cash (or \"yellow cash\") and \"red cash\" was set at 10 standard cash coins were worth 1 \"red cash coin\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Xiaoping Qian (小平錢) refers to the smallest and most common cash coins, they usually had a diameter of about 2.4–2.5 cm and weights between 3–4 grams."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The last Chinese cash coins in Indonesia circulated in Bali until 1970 and are still used for most Hindu rituals today."]], "year": 0},
